; SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
;
; Node — the syntax tree, ported from the reference's internal/ast/ast.go and
; ast_generated.go.
;
; ── The shape, and why it is this one (CLAUDE.md §3.1) ──────────────────────
;
; The reference's Node is a tag-struct with an INTERFACE payload:
;
;     type Node struct { Kind Kind; Flags NodeFlags; Loc TextRange; data nodeData }
;
; where `nodeData` has ~20 methods and 168 concrete implementations. Here the
; interface becomes a SUM TYPE — `NodeData union` — and every dispatch is a
; `choose`. The reason is not taste: a trait object makes the callee unknown at
; compile time, and purity, escape and parallelism analysis all stop at that
; point. Upstream says the same thing from the other side at Node.IterChildren
; ("an interface call is opaque to escape analysis").
;
; The SPLIT is kept exactly as the reference has it — kind/flags/pos/end beside
; the payload, not folded into the union. That is load-bearing rather than
; imitative: many KINDS share one payload. Every punctuation and keyword token
; node is a `Token` with no fields at all, so a union arm per kind would be 100+
; empty arms and the kind would still have to be stored to tell them apart.
;
; ★ ORDER-OF-DECLARATION AUFLAGE, inherited from dazzle's `Insn union`: the tag
; IS the jump-table index, and AArch64's MinimumJumpTableEntries is 13 — a
; dispatch level needs >= 14 CONTIGUOUS tags or LLVM lowers a compare chain
; instead. That does not bite yet (this slice has twelve arms and every choose
; over them is cold), but the union only grows, and the arms that end up on the
; hot path must be contiguous when it does. Do not reorder for tidiness.
;
; ── What is carried ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Slices 1–2: SourceFile, the bare Token, Identifier, the literal kinds, and the
; statements a file of declarations and expressions is made of. Slice 3 adds the
; TYPE surface — the alias declaration, type parameters, references and
; qualified names, unions and intersections, the postfix and bracketed forms,
; tuples, conditional and infer types, and the predicates. Slice 4 adds the
; SIGNATURE — the parameter, the function declaration, the function and
; constructor types, and the two statements a function BODY is made of that a
; file of declarations never needed: `return` and `if`. Slice 5 adds the
; EXPRESSION ladder: the binary and conditional operators, the unary and update
; forms, member/element access and calls, `as`/`satisfies`, the parenthesized,
; array and object literals, `new`, the function expression and the arrow
; function. Slice 6 adds the CLASS family — the declaration and the expression,
; the heritage clauses, and the six member forms (method, property,
; constructor, get/set accessor, static block, index signature). Slice 7 adds
; the TYPE-MEMBER surface — the object type literal and the five member forms
; an interface body is made of, the mapped type, and the interface, enum and
; module declarations. Slice 8 adds the IMPORT and EXPORT declarations, which
; is parseDeclarationWorker's last two arms and therefore the last of the
; router. Slice 9 adds the LOOP and BRANCH statements — `do`, `while`, the
; three `for` heads, `break`/`continue`, `with`, `switch` with its case block
; and clauses, `throw`, `try`/`catch`/`finally`, `debugger`, and the labeled
; statement that `break outer` exists for. Slice 10 adds the BINDING PATTERN,
; slice 11 the TEMPLATE family, and slice 12 the DECORATOR — ONE arm, because a
; decorator is an element of the same `modifiers` list a keyword goes into and
; needed no new slot on any node that carries one.
;
; Everything else is not a missing arm here — it is a construct the parser
; reports as unported before it ever gets to build a node.
;
; ★ The union is now 134 arms, and the AUFLAGE above starts to bite: the arms
; that end up on a hot path must be CONTIGUOUS. Nothing here is hot yet (the
; only consumer is a debug walk), so no order is being defended — but the next
; reader to add an arm should add it at the END rather than "where it belongs
; alphabetically", so that a later regrouping is one deliberate move instead of
; an archaeology exercise.
;
; ★ Three node kinds deliberately have NO arm of their own: OmittedExpression,
; the zero-width placeholder an array literal's elision leaves behind (`[, 1]`),
; SemicolonClassElement, the stray `;` between class members, and (slice 9)
; DebuggerStatement. None of the three carries a field or a child, so each is a
; `Token` whose KIND says which it is — the same reasoning KeywordTypeNode and
; ThisTypeNode are Tokens under.
;
; NOT carried, and each omission is a decision rather than an oversight:
;
;   - `Parent`, and this entry was WRONG until slice 26. It said the reference
;     "sets it in a pass AFTER ParseSourceFile (setParentFromContext)"; at this
;     pin `finishNodeWithEnd` ends in `overrideParentInImmediateChildren`, so
;     every node gets its Parent DURING the parse, from the finishNode of the
;     node that owns it. What is still true is that there is nothing to compare:
;     MEASURED over the 12 444 files of the stage-2 corpus (1 467 387 nodes) and
;     all 296 fixtures, every node's Parent is exactly its parent in the
;     ForEachChild walk and only the SourceFile's is nil — the reference's four
;     "force reset" sites are what make that hold rather than luck — so a parent
;     column in the ast dump would compare a constant. Its first READER is the
;     binder, which asks `node.Parent` everywhere, and the binder yardstick is
;     where a wrong parent becomes a wrong symbol table. Under RBMM the
;     back-pointer costs nothing when it comes: a cycle inside one region is
;     freed with the region.
;   - The node ID. It is assigned deterministically at parse time here, not
;     lazily by CAS into the shared tree (CLAUDE.md §3.10) — but nothing reads
;     one yet, so the field waits for its first reader.
;   - The LITERAL text, and only that: slice 17 gave the scanner a tokenValue
;     and gave the IDENTIFIER its text, because the parser itself reads that one
;     back. A literal's text is read by the checker and the emitter, neither of
;     which this port has reached, and neither yardstick compares a node's text —
;     so a stored one could not be checked. Making it observable is a DUMP
;     change before it is a parser one, and it drags the NUMERIC value in with
;     it (see the scanner's set_numeric_token_value).

use scaly.memory.Page
use scaly.containers.Array
use scaly.containers.Vector
use tscaly.Diagnostics.DiagnosticList

; ── per-kind payload records ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; One record per reference struct, named for it. A record with no fields is not
; a placeholder: the reference's Token and EmptyStatement have none either.

define TokenData ()

; ★★★ Slice 17. The reference's `Identifier{ Text string }` — and the only node
; text this port stores, because it is the only one the PARSER itself reads
; back: parseErrorForMissingSemicolonAfter asks node.Text() to decide which
; diagnostic a missing semicolon gets, and that answer is compared by the
; yardstick. A literal's text is read by the checker and the emitter, which this
; port has not reached, so LiteralData stays empty and says so at its own line.
;
; A pointer and a length, the same pair and for the same reason as the scanner's
; token value — a Go string is exactly that, and an identifier's text is either a
; slice of the source or a decode the scanner already built.
;
; ★ It is NOT shared with PrivateIdentifier by accident: the reference gives
; both of them a bare `Text string` and nothing else, so the slot lists are
; identical, which is §3.5n's condition. A SYNTHESIZED identifier — the missing
; one, and the stand-in an import specifier gets for a refused name — carries
; (null, 0), which is the reference's `p.newIdentifier("")`.
define IdentifierData
(
    text: pointer[char]
    text_len: int
)
{
    init(t: pointer[char], n: int)
    {
        set text: t
        set text_len: n
    }
}

; ★★★ Slice 31. THE TEXT IS STORED, and what changed is not the dump — it is
; that a literal's text became a SYMBOL NAME. What stood here said "no reader in
; this port asks, and neither yardstick compares a node's text, so a stored one
; could not be checked", and both halves were true until the module slice:
; getDeclarationName answers `"` + name.Text() + `"` for an AMBIENT MODULE, so
; `declare module "foo"` declares a symbol called `"foo"` — a name the symbols
; yardstick prints and compares. That is §3.5be read forwards, the way slice 29
; read it for is_declaration_file: the slice that has the reader is the slice
; that adds the field.
;
; It is a pointer and a length, filled from `p.scanner.TokenValue()` at the node
; factory exactly as IdentifierData's is, so the DECODED value is stored (an
; escape is resolved by the scanner) and nothing is copied.
;
; ★ The RawText of the four template-literal-like nodes is still NOT stored, and
; that argument survives intact: its readers are the checker and the emitter, and
; no name, span or diagnostic here can be a function of it.
;
; ★★★ TOKEN FLAGS ARE STORED, since slice 19, and one arm of one reader is what
; earns them: `isDoubleQuotedString` — StringLiteral with SingleQuote CLEAR — is
; how parseJsonText decides whether a JSON key or value gets error 1327, and a
; diagnostic IS compared. That is the whole of §3.5ao's demand: name the reader
; that makes a stored field observable.
;
; ★ ONE record for every literal, as upstream: `LiteralExpressionBase` carries
; Text and TokenFlags for all of them, so §3.5n's test — read the reference's
; struct, share only when the field lists match — passes, and splitting the
; string literal out because it is the only arm with a reader today would be a
; divergence made for a reason §3.5n says is not the test.
;
; ★ The VALUE is masked per kind, exactly as each factory masks it
; (`tokenFlags & TokenFlagsStringLiteralFlags` and the three siblings). So the
; arms other than StringLiteral carry the reference's own value and no reader:
; ungated, stated here rather than left to be discovered.
define LiteralData
(
    text: pointer[char]
    text_len: int
    token_flags: int
)
{
    init(t: pointer[char], n: int, f: int)
    {
        set text: t
        set text_len: n
        set token_flags: f
    }
}
define EmptyStatementData ()

; ── the list-range side table's row ─────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★ One row of the side table that carries what a NodeList's own TextRange
; carries upstream — see `list_ranges` on the Parser for why the range lives
; beside the list rather than in it, and §3.5bq for the measurement that
; rejected deriving it from the elements. Three fields and no methods; the
; SEARCH is a static below, because it now has two callers in two phases.
;
; ★★★ IT LIVES HERE RATHER THAN ON THE PARSER BECAUSE THE CHECKER READS IT.
; Slice 24 built it for three JS-syntax arms of the parser itself, so the table
; was a parser field and the row a parser type. Slice 51's grammar checks ask
; the same question one phase later — `HasTrailingComma()` is
; `last.End() < list.End()`, and an EMPTY bracketed list reports at a span
; nothing but the list itself knows — and a Checker holds no Parser. So the
; table is handed over on the SourceFile, exactly as the two diagnostic lists
; are, and this record moves to the module that both phases already read.
define ListRange
(
    list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    pos: int
    end: int
)
{
    init(l: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]], p: int, e: int)
    {
        set list: l
        set pos: p
        set end: e
    }
}

; ── SourceFile.CheckJsDirective, as a tristate (checker slice 69) ────────────
;
; ast.CheckJsDirective is a POINTER to a record carrying `Enabled`, so the slot
; has three states and IsCheckJSEnabledForFile reads all three. The names are
; here rather than in the checker because the PARSER writes them.
define CheckJsDirectiveNone: int 0
define CheckJsDirectiveEnabled: int 1
define CheckJsDirectiveDisabled: int 2

define SourceFileData
(
    statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    end_of_file_token: ref[AstNode]?

    ; The reference's `result.SetDiagnostics(...)` in finishSourceFile. The
    ; parser accumulates into this list during the parse and hands it over at the
    ; end, which is also what makes the list readable from a program ROOT: a
    ; generic instance handed across a package boundary reads back as garbage
    ; (root CLAUDE.md), so the dump asks AstNode for it through statics, exactly
    ; as it asks for children.
    ;
    ; ★ NOT a child. It is absent from this arm's slot list in child_at below,
    ; because ForEachChild does not visit diagnostics — adding it there would put
    ; a diagnostic in the tree walk and every case would diverge.
    diagnostics: ref[DiagnosticList]?

    ; ★★★ The reference's `result.SetJSDiagnostics(...)`, and a SECOND list rather
    ; than more entries in the first because `sf.Diagnostics()` does not include
    ; it. checkJSSyntax reports TypeScript-only syntax found in a JavaScript file
    ; into it; a port that merged the two would put those diagnostics in the D
    ; section, where the reference has none, and every JavaScript unit would
    ; diverge. Not a child either, for the reason the list above is not.
    ;
    ; ★ It is null for a file that is not JavaScript — finishSourceFile guards the
    ; assignment with `ast.IsInJSFile(node)` — and the dump prints nothing for a
    ; null list, which is what makes an empty J section a comparison.
    js_diagnostics: ref[DiagnosticList]?

    ; ★★★ The LIST-RANGE side table, handed over by the parser the way the two
    ; lists above are. Upstream has no such slot because upstream's NodeList
    ; carries its own TextRange; here the range is beside the list (see
    ; ListRange), the table was a parser field while the parser was its only
    ; reader, and the CHECKER's grammar checks are the second reader — a Checker
    ; holds no Parser, so the table has to travel on the tree.
    ;
    ; ★ Not a child, for the reason neither diagnostic list is one: ForEachChild
    ; visits a list's ELEMENTS, and a list's extent is not a node. It is also not
    ; DUMPED anywhere — no yardstick can see a list's range in either direction,
    ; which is exactly what §3.5bq says and is why the table needed a reader
    ; before it could be trusted at all.
    ;
    ; ★ The two SourceFile nodes a top-level-await reparse produces get the SAME
    ; table, because it is the same parse: a rewind leaves an unreachable row
    ; rather than a wrong one (the key is a freshly allocated list), so one table
    ; per parse is one table per file here.
    list_ranges: ref[Array[ListRange]]

    ; ★★★ SourceFile.CheckJsDirective, arriving in checker slice 69 — and the
    ; parser's own note at extract_pragmas is what dates it: it said the field's
    ; *"reader is the checker"* and dropped the pragma on that ground. The reader
    ; exists now (reportImplicitAny returns without a word in a JavaScript file
    ; unless checkJs is on for it), so the field is stored rather than named.
    ;
    ; ★★ A TRISTATE, not a boolean: upstream's slot is a POINTER to a record with
    ; an `Enabled` flag, so *absent* and *present and disabled* are different
    ; answers and IsCheckJSEnabledForFile reads the difference — absent falls
    ; through to the compiler option, present does not (§3.5j).
    ;
    ; ★ Not a child, and not dumped, for the reason neither diagnostic list nor
    ; the list-range table is: ForEachChild does not visit it, and no yardstick can
    ; see it in either direction. Its gate is the diagnostic it withholds.
    check_js_directive: int
)

define BlockData (statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)

define ExpressionStatementData (expression: ref[AstNode]?)

define VariableStatementData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    declaration_list: ref[AstNode]
)

define VariableDeclarationListData (declarations: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)

; The expression is OPTIONAL — a bare `return;` has none — which is why this is
; not ExpressionStatementData under another arm name despite the identical slot
; list: the two records differ in what null MEANS, and that difference is the
; kind of thing a shared record hides.
define ReturnStatementData (expression: ref[AstNode]?)

define IfStatementData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    then_statement: ref[AstNode]
    else_statement: ref[AstNode]?
)

define VariableDeclarationData
(
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    exclamation_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── the type surface (parser slice 3) ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★ TWO reference node types need NO record of their own, and saying so is
; cheaper than a comment on each absence:
;
;   KeywordTypeNode  — `string`, `number`, `any`, `void`, … It carries nothing
;                      but its KIND, which is the keyword's own kind, and the
;                      kind lives beside the payload rather than in it (see the
;                      header). So it is a `Token`, exactly as a punctuation
;                      node is.
;   ThisTypeNode     — likewise: kind KindThisType, no fields, no children.
;
; Several arms deliberately SHARE a record. `WrappedTypeData` is the reference's
; recurring one-child type node under one name; the ARM tells the kinds apart,
; and the kind is what the yardstick compares. Grouping them is not a
; simplification of the reference — each of these structs really does have
; exactly one child slot, and the arm names keep them individually visible.

define WrappedTypeData (inner: ref[AstNode]?)

; ★★★ THE ONE-CHILD TYPE NODE THAT IS NOT ONE, AND SLICE 61 IS THE SLICE THAT
; PAID FOR IT. A TypeOperatorNode carries WHICH operator it is — `keyof`,
; `unique` or `readonly` — and this port shared WrappedTypeData with the others
; and dropped it, which parser slice 7's faithfulness note (1) named as a real
; gap rather than a saving: the ast yardstick compares kind, range and flags, so
; a wrong operator would have been invisible. checkGrammarTypeOperatorNode is the
; first READER — its whole body is a switch on the operator — and note (1) says
; the slice that first reads such a field is the slice that adds it.
;
; ★ The slot is a Kind and not a node, so it is NOT in `child_at`: the reference's
; ForEachChild for a TypeOperatorNode visits `Type` and nothing else.
;
; ★ `operator_kind` rather than the reference's `Operator`, and it is §3.5d's
; reserved word met a second time — the first was a PARAMETER named `operator` and
; this is a FIELD; PrefixUnaryExpressionData has carried the same rename since the
; expression ladder landed, which is what settles the spelling.
define TypeOperatorData
(
    operator_kind: int
    inner: ref[AstNode]
)

define TypeListData (types: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]])

define QualifiedNameData
(
    left: ref[AstNode]
    right: ref[AstNode]?
)

define TypeReferenceData
(
    type_name: ref[AstNode]
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define TypeQueryData
(
    expr_name: ref[AstNode]
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define IndexedAccessTypeData
(
    object_type: ref[AstNode]
    index_type: ref[AstNode]
)

define ConditionalTypeData
(
    check_type: ref[AstNode]
    extends_type: ref[AstNode]
    true_type: ref[AstNode]
    false_type: ref[AstNode]
)

define TypePredicateData
(
    asserts_modifier: ref[AstNode]?
    parameter_name: ref[AstNode]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

define NamedTupleMemberData
(
    dot_dot_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    question_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

define TypeParameterData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    constraint: ref[AstNode]?
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    default_type: ref[AstNode]?
)

define TypeAliasDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── signatures (parser slice 4) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The reference's ParameterDeclaration, FunctionDeclaration, FunctionTypeNode
; and ConstructorTypeNode. They share the SIGNATURE shape — type parameters,
; parameters, return type — and they are separate records anyway, because the
; slots around that shape differ and the slot ORDER is what the yardstick
; compares.

define ParameterData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    dot_dot_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    question_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ The reference has a slot this record does not: `FullSignature`, visited
; between `Type` and `Body`. It is only ever non-nil for a JSDoc reparse — the
; parse path passes nil at its single construction site — so it is left out
; rather than carried as a field nothing can ever fill. The slice that ports
; the JSDoc reparser adds it, and its place in the child order is HERE, before
; the body.
define FunctionDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    asterisk_token: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
    body: ref[AstNode]?
)

define FunctionTypeData
(
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

define ConstructorTypeData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── the expression ladder (parser slice 5) ──────────────────────────────────
;
; ★ THREE reference structs carry a slot this port does not, and all three are
; nil on the PARSE path — they exist for the JSDoc reparser, which builds a
; BinaryExpression / PropertyAssignment / ShorthandPropertyAssignment with a
; type annotation that no source text can produce:
;
;   BinaryExpression              modifiers, Type   (NewBinaryExpression is
;                                                    called with nil for both)
;   PropertyAssignment            Type
;   ShorthandPropertyAssignment   Type
;
; Same treatment as FunctionDeclaration's FullSignature above: left out rather
; than carried as a field nothing can fill, and the place in the child order is
; named here so the JSDoc slice can put it back — Type sits BETWEEN Left and
; OperatorToken, and between the postfix token and what follows it.
;
; ★ ArrayLiteralExpression and ObjectLiteralExpression additionally carry a
; `MultiLine bool`. It is a plain FIELD, not a NodeFlag, so the yardstick cannot
; see it and nothing here reads one — stated rather than silently dropped,
; because a distinction the dump hides hides every bug in it.

; ★★★ NOT WrappedTypeData, and slice 19 is where that stopped being true. The
; reference's struct is `{ Operator Kind; Operand *Expression }` — two fields,
; where every WrappedTypeData sharer has one — and the extra field is not a
; child, so no walk could ever have shown the difference. What shows it is a
; READER: validateJsonValue admits a PrefixUnaryExpression only when its
; operator is MINUS and its operand a numeric literal, so `{"a": +1}` is error
; 1328 and `{"a": -1}` is valid JSON. §3.5an from the other side — a deferred
; field defers the DISTINCTIONS it makes, and this one came back as a folded arm.
;
; `operator_kind`, not `operator`: §3.5d — `operator` is a declaration keyword
; and cannot be a parameter name, and a field would read as one at every init.
define PrefixUnaryExpressionData
(
    operator_kind: int
    operand: ref[AstNode]?
)

define BinaryExpressionData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    left: ref[AstNode]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    operator_token: ref[AstNode]
    right: ref[AstNode]?
)

define ConditionalExpressionData
(
    condition: ref[AstNode]
    question_token: ref[AstNode]
    when_true: ref[AstNode]
    colon_token: ref[AstNode]
    when_false: ref[AstNode]
)

; asteriskToken, expression — both optional, since a bare `yield;` has neither.
define YieldExpressionData
(
    asterisk_token: ref[AstNode]?
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
)

; AsExpression and SatisfiesExpression: expression, then type. Shared, and the
; ARM tells them apart — the same reasoning WrappedTypeData is shared under.
define AsExpressionData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; `<T>x`. The type comes FIRST here, which is the opposite of AsExpression and
; is exactly why the two do not share a record: the order is what the yardstick
; compares.
define TypeAssertionData
(
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
)

define PropertyAccessData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    question_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
)

define ElementAccessData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    question_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    argument_expression: ref[AstNode]
)

define CallExpressionData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    question_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
)

define NewExpressionData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define ExpressionWithTypeArgumentsData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; The elements of an array literal and the properties of an object literal. A
; separate record from TypeListData above only because the two say different
; things about what they hold; both are one list and nothing else.
define ElementListData (elements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]])

define ArrowFunctionData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
    equals_greater_than_token: ref[AstNode]
    body: ref[AstNode]
)

define FunctionExpressionData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    asterisk_token: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
    body: ref[AstNode]
)

define PropertyAssignmentData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

define ShorthandPropertyAssignmentData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    equals_token: ref[AstNode]?
    object_assignment_initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── the class family (parser slice 6) ───────────────────────────────────────

; ClassDeclaration and ClassExpression have the SAME slot list, in the same
; order, and the reference gives them one embedded ClassLikeBase for exactly
; that reason. The ARM tells them apart, as it does for AsExpression and
; SatisfiesExpression.
define ClassLikeData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    heritage_clauses: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    members: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; ★ `token` is the KEYWORD — KindExtendsKeyword or KindImplementsKeyword — and
; it is a field rather than a child: the reference stores it as a bare
; `Token ast.Kind` and its ForEachChild visits only Types. The node's own
; `kind` is KindHeritageClause for both, so without this field the two clauses
; would be indistinguishable. Nothing reads it yet and the yardstick cannot
; see it, but a clause that does not know which one it is is not a port of
; this node.
define HeritageClauseData
(
    token: int
    types: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
)

define MethodDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    asterisk_token: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
    body: ref[AstNode]?
)

define PropertyDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

; A constructor has no NAME — `constructor` is the keyword, not an identifier
; node — which is the one thing that keeps this from being MethodDeclarationData.
define ConstructorDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
    body: ref[AstNode]?
)

; GetAccessorDeclaration and SetAccessorDeclaration, again one record and two
; arms: the reference's two ForEachChild bodies are slot-for-slot identical.
define AccessorDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
    body: ref[AstNode]?
)

define ClassStaticBlockDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    body: ref[AstNode]
)

define IndexSignatureDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?

    ; ★★★ `full_signature` is on these four for the same reason it is on the
    ; declaration kinds — the reference's FunctionLikeBase carries it and the
    ; JSDoc reparser's `@type` arm fills it through getFunctionLikeHost, which
    ; answers a signature MEMBER when that is the node the comment documents —
    ; but it is deliberately NOT in `child_at`, because the reference's
    ; ForEachChild for these four does not visit it either. Its one reader is the
    ; GUARD in the @template / @param / @returns arms: a signature that already
    ; has a full signature takes no further tag. A field with no visible effect
    ; and exactly one reader (§3.5ao) — do not "complete" the child list with it.
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── the type-member surface (parser slice 7) ────────────────────────────────
;
; An interface BODY and an object TYPE LITERAL are the same production —
; parseTypeMember, with its five member forms — which is why they are one
; slice and one set of records.

define TypeLiteralData (members: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?)

; CallSignature and ConstructSignature, one record and two arms: the
; reference's two ForEachChild bodies are slot-for-slot identical, and the
; `new` of a construct signature is a keyword rather than a child.
define SignatureDeclarationData
(
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?

    ; ★★★ `full_signature` is on these four for the same reason it is on the
    ; declaration kinds — the reference's FunctionLikeBase carries it and the
    ; JSDoc reparser's `@type` arm fills it through getFunctionLikeHost, which
    ; answers a signature MEMBER when that is the node the comment documents —
    ; but it is deliberately NOT in `child_at`, because the reference's
    ; ForEachChild for these four does not visit it either. Its one reader is the
    ; GUARD in the @template / @param / @returns arms: a signature that already
    ; has a full signature takes no further tag. A field with no visible effect
    ; and exactly one reader (§3.5ao) — do not "complete" the child list with it.
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ NOT MethodDeclarationData. The two field LISTS differ by an asterisk and a
; body, and it is tempting to reuse the record with both left null, because
; the visit order that comes out is then the same. It is the same by accident:
; a generator method signature is a grammar error the CHECKER reports, so the
; reference PARSES the asterisk in a class and refuses it here by having no
; slot for it. A shared record would give this node somewhere to put one.
define MethodSignatureData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    postfix_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?

    ; ★★★ `full_signature` is on these four for the same reason it is on the
    ; declaration kinds — the reference's FunctionLikeBase carries it and the
    ; JSDoc reparser's `@type` arm fills it through getFunctionLikeHost, which
    ; answers a signature MEMBER when that is the node the comment documents —
    ; but it is deliberately NOT in `child_at`, because the reference's
    ; ForEachChild for these four does not visit it either. Its one reader is the
    ; GUARD in the @template / @param / @returns arms: a signature that already
    ; has a full signature takes no further tag. A field with no visible effect
    ; and exactly one reader (§3.5ao) — do not "complete" the child list with it.
    full_signature: ref[AstNode]?
)

define EnumDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    members: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define EnumMemberData
(
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ `keyword` is KindNamespaceKeyword, KindModuleKeyword or KindGlobalKeyword,
; and it is a field rather than a child — the same shape HeritageClauseData's
; `token` has, for the same reason. All three spellings produce a node whose
; own `kind` is KindModuleDeclaration, so without this field `namespace A {}`
; and `module A {}` are indistinguishable. The yardstick cannot see it; a
; module that does not know which word declared it is not a port of this node.
define ModuleDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    keyword: int
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    body: ref[AstNode]?
)

define MappedTypeData
(
    readonly_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameter: ref[AstNode]
    name_type: ref[AstNode]?
    question_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    members: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; ── the import and export declarations (parser slice 8) ─────────────────────
;
; parseDeclarationWorker's two remaining arms. They are one slice because they
; are one production in three places: parseImportOrExportSpecifier builds
; `{ a as b }` for both sides, parseModuleSpecifier reads the `"mod"` for both,
; and parseImportAttributes reads the `with { … }` for both.

define ImportDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    import_clause: ref[AstNode]?
    module_specifier: ref[AstNode]?
    attributes: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ `is_type_only` is a FIELD, not a child — the shape HeritageClauseData's
; `token` and ModuleDeclarationData's `keyword` have, and for the same reason:
; `import type A = require("m")` and `import A = require("m")` produce the same
; kind with the same children, so without the field the two are the same node.
define ImportEqualsDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    is_type_only: bool
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    module_reference: ref[AstNode]
)

; ★ `phase_modifier` is the reference's ImportPhaseModifierSyntaxKind —
; KindTypeKeyword, KindDeferKeyword, or KindUnknown for a plain import. A
; field, for the reason above.
define ImportClauseData
(
    phase_modifier: int
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    named_bindings: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ImportSpecifier and ExportSpecifier, one record and two arms — and this is
; the §3.5n check performed rather than assumed: the reference's two structs
; carry the SAME three slots in the same order (IsTypeOnly, PropertyName,
; name) and their two ForEachChild bodies are identical. The only difference
; is a Go TYPE — an import specifier's name is an IdentifierNode and an export
; specifier's a ModuleExportName, i.e. an identifier OR a string literal —
; which is a distinction the parser enforces at parse_import_specifier, not a
; slot this record would have to hold.
define ImportOrExportSpecifierData
(
    is_type_only: bool
    property_name: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ `token` is KindWithKeyword or KindAssertKeyword: `with { type: "json" }`
; and the deprecated `assert { … }` are the same node under different words,
; so the word is the only thing that tells them apart. (Only the `with` form
; can reach this port — the reference reports a diagnostic on the other.)
;
; ★ The reference also carries `MultiLine bool`, a formatting hint the
; yardstick cannot see and nothing here reads — left out with the same
; statement ArrayLiteralExpression's MultiLine gets, rather than silently.
define ImportAttributesData
(
    token: int
    attributes: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define ImportAttributeData
(
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    value: ref[AstNode]
)

; ★ modifiers, [type], expression — the bracketed slot is the JSDoc
; reparser's, exactly as FunctionDeclaration's FullSignature is:
; NewExportAssignment is called with nil for it at its one parse-path site.
; `export = x` and `export default x` are one kind, so which word was written
; rides as a field.
define ExportAssignmentData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    is_export_equals: bool
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
)

define NamespaceExportDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
)

; NOT ImportDeclarationData, although four of the five slots line up: the
; reference's ExportDeclaration carries IsTypeOnly and ImportDeclaration does
; not (`import type x from "m"` puts the phase on the CLAUSE instead). The
; §3.5n rule again — read both structs, share only on an identical list.
define ExportDeclarationData
(
    modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    is_type_only: bool
    export_clause: ref[AstNode]?
    module_specifier: ref[AstNode]?
    attributes: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── slice 9: the loop and branch statements ─────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ A RULE THIS SLICE HAD TO STATE, because `do` and `while` are the case
; that forces it: the reference gives both of them the SAME two slots — an
; expression and a controlled statement, out of IterationStatementBase plus
; one field — and visits them in OPPOSITE orders (`do` runs its body before it
; evaluates the condition, and ForEachChild says so). §3.5n's test is that the
; slot LIST is identical, and an order is part of a list. So the invariant here
; is stronger than "same fields ⇒ share": **a record's field order IS its visit
; order**, which is what makes a child_at arm checkable line by line against
; ast_generated.go. `do` therefore gets its own record even though its fields
; are `while`'s two, and WithStatement shares `while`'s because its order
; matches as well as its fields.
define DoStatementData
(
    statement: ref[AstNode]
    expression: ref[AstNode]
)

; WhileStatement and WithStatement: expression, then statement. The name is the
; shape rather than either node, the way WrappedTypeData and ElementListData
; are named — see the rule above.
define ControlledStatementData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]
    statement: ref[AstNode]
)

define ForStatementData
(
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
    condition: ref[AstNode]?
    incrementor: ref[AstNode]?
    statement: ref[AstNode]
)

; ForInStatement and ForOfStatement are ONE reference struct
; (ForInOrOfStatement), so this is the §3.5n check passing rather than a
; resemblance. `await_modifier` is only ever present on the `of` form — the
; reference builds the `in` form with nil for it — but the slot belongs to the
; struct, not to the kind.
define ForInOrOfStatementData
(
    await_modifier: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]
    expression: ref[AstNode]
    statement: ref[AstNode]
)

; BreakStatement and ContinueStatement: one optional label, and the
; reference's two structs are field for field the same.
;
; ★ The slot is `label_name`, not `label`: `label` is a RESERVED WORD in Scaly
; and it bites a STRUCTURE PROPERTY exactly as §3.5d found it biting a
; parameter — the parse fails at the property's own column with
; `expected ')'`, saying nothing about reservation, so it reads as a
; mis-balanced record. The root CLAUDE.md lists `label` among the words that
; cannot be BINDING names; the rule is wider than that list's wording.
define BreakOrContinueStatementData (label_name: ref[AstNode]?)

define LabeledStatementData
(
    label_name: ref[AstNode]
    statement: ref[AstNode]
)

define SwitchStatementData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]
    case_block: ref[AstNode]
)

; CaseClause and DefaultClause are ONE reference struct (CaseOrDefaultClause).
; The expression is absent on the default form, which is the same
; "null MEANS something here" the ReturnStatementData note is about — but it
; is one struct upstream, so the two arms share the record and the KIND is
; what says whether the slot can be filled.
define CaseOrDefaultClauseData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
    statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define TryStatementData
(
    try_block: ref[AstNode]
    catch_clause: ref[AstNode]?
    finally_block: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ NO jsdoc slot and no modifiers — and the absence of the jsdoc CALL in
; parse_catch_clause is the thing to preserve: it is the one clause-shaped node
; in the reference whose parse routine does not call withJSDoc, while every
; statement around it does. Ported as written.
define CatchClauseData
(
    variable_declaration: ref[AstNode]?
    block: ref[AstNode]
)

; ── slice 10: the binding pattern ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ObjectBindingPattern and ArrayBindingPattern are ONE reference struct
; (BindingPattern, whose only field is the element list), so they share a
; record — and the record they share is ElementListData, which is the §3.5n
; test passing rather than a resemblance: one list and nothing else, the same
; reading CaseBlock and NamedImports are under. The KIND is what says which
; bracket was written.
;
; ★ The elements of the two are NOT the same node, though: an array pattern's
; are BindingElements with a null propertyName, an object pattern's may carry
; one. That difference lives in the element, not in the pattern, which is why
; one record serves both here.

; DotDotDotToken, PropertyName, name, Initializer — all four OPTIONAL, and all
; four null at once for an ELISION: `[, a]` gives the reference a
; zero-width BindingElement with nothing in it, not the OmittedExpression an
; array LITERAL's hole gets. Two node kinds for the same hole in two grammars,
; which is exactly the kind of thing a shared record would have hidden.
define BindingElementData
(
    dot_dot_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    property_name: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── slice 11: the template family ───────────────────────────────────────────
;
; TemplateExpression and TemplateLiteralType are ONE record, and the §3.5n check
; is what says so rather than the resemblance: both reference structs are
; `Head *TemplateHeadNode` followed by `TemplateSpans *…List`, two slots in that
; order. The list's ELEMENT type differs (TemplateSpan against
; TemplateLiteralTypeSpan) and that difference is not expressible here — a list
; is an Array of AstNode either way — which is the same reading NamedImports and
; NamedExports already share ElementListData under, and ClassDeclaration and
; InterfaceDeclaration share ClassLikeData under.
define TemplateLiteralData
(
    head: ref[AstNode]?
    template_spans: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
)

; TemplateSpan and TemplateLiteralTypeSpan, likewise one record: two slots, in
; the same order, neither ever null. The reference names slot 0 `Expression` in
; the one and `Type` in the other, and a NAME is not part of the §3.5n test —
; WrappedTypeData has been shared by fourteen arms whose reference field names
; are all different since slice 3.
;
; ★ The `literal` slot is what the span LOOP reads: a span whose literal is a
; TemplateMiddle is followed by another span, and one whose literal is a
; TemplateTail ends the template. That is why template_span_literal exists as an
; accessor rather than the loop reaching for child index 1 — the index is only
; right while the expression slot happens to be non-null.
define TemplateSpanData
(
    expression: ref[AstNode]
    literal: ref[AstNode]
)

; tag, questionDotToken, typeArguments, template — in that order, and the
; type-argument LIST sits third, between two single slots.
define TaggedTemplateData
(
    tag: ref[AstNode]
    question_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    template: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ── slice 18: the import in a position that is not a declaration ────────────
;
; ★ `keyword_token` is the reference's `MetaProperty.KeywordToken` — KindNewKeyword
; for `new.target`, KindImportKeyword for `import.meta` — and it was the field
; §3.5's faithfulness note named as "the keyword of a MetaProperty … not stored,
; and that is a gap rather than a saving. Stated so that the slice which first
; READS such a field knows to add it." This is that slice: the external-module
; indicator's last arm is `IsImportMeta`, which is exactly
; `KeywordToken == KindImportKeyword && Name().Text() == "meta"`, so the two
; spellings of a MetaProperty had to become tellable apart before a file could be
; called a module because of one. §3.5an is the general shape — a deferred field
; defers the DISTINCTIONS it makes, and they come back as folded arms.
define MetaPropertyData
(
    keyword_token: int
    name: ref[AstNode]?
)

; isTypeOf, argument, [attributes], [qualifier], typeArguments — the reference's
; ForEachChild visits the last four in that order and never the first.
;
; ★ `is_type_of` is a FIELD, and it is stored for the reason
; ImportEqualsDeclarationData's `is_type_only` is: `typeof import("m")` and
; `import("m")` produce the same kind with the same children, so without it the
; two are the same node. ★★ It was stored knowing the yardstick could not SEE
; it — §3.5ao's report rather than a silent gap: the dump compares kind, range and
; flags, so a wrong value here would have passed — and the reader that note named
; is the CHECKER. ★★★ SLICE 47 IS THAT READER: is_part_of_type_node_in_parent asks
; `!parent.IsTypeOf` for a node under an ImportType, which decides whether the node
; appears in the fifth yardstick's T section at all. So the field is observable
; now, through the artifact the note predicted, and the deferral is closed rather
; than still open.
define ImportTypeData
(
    is_type_of: bool
    argument: ref[AstNode]
    attributes: ref[AstNode]?
    qualifier: ref[AstNode]?
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; ── slice 20: the JSX node records ──────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Eleven kinds, seven records. Four of them reuse an existing record and each
; reuse is checked against ast_generated.go the way §3.5n demands rather than by
; resemblance: JsxClosingElement and JsxSpreadAttribute have exactly ONE child
; slot each (WrappedTypeData — the tag name and the spread's expression),
; JsxAttributes has exactly one LIST (ElementListData), and JsxSelfClosingElement
; shares JsxOpeningElementData because the reference's two structs carry the same
; three slots in the same order.
;
; ★ JsxOpeningFragment and JsxClosingFragment get NO record and no arm: `<>` and
; `</>` carry no field and no child, so they are a `Token` whose KIND says which
; it is — the reading DebuggerStatement, OmittedExpression and
; SemicolonClassElement are already under.

; ★★★ `children_end` is NOT a slot of the reference's struct — it is the END of
; its `Children *JsxChildList`, i.e. the TextRange a NodeList carries and this
; port's lists do not (see AstNode.new_node_list). It has to be kept for exactly
; one reader, and that reader is the reason it cannot be derived at the point of
; use: parseJsxElementOrSelfClosingElementOrFragment reads
; `lastChild.Children().End()` off a child it FINISHED an unknown number of
; tokens ago, to place a synthesized closing tag there.
;
; ★ It is not a child and takes no part in child_at — the reference's ForEachChild
; visits the list's ELEMENTS, and a list's extent is not a node.
define JsxElementData
(
    opening_element: ref[AstNode]
    children: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    closing_element: ref[AstNode]?
    children_end: int
)

define JsxOpeningElementData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    type_arguments: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    attributes: ref[AstNode]
)

define JsxFragmentData
(
    opening_fragment: ref[AstNode]
    children: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    closing_fragment: ref[AstNode]
)

; ★★★ A LEAF WHOSE KIND DOES NOT SURVIVE, which is why the field is here at all.
; The scanner answers JsxText or JsxTextAllWhiteSpaces, and
; `NewJsxText(text, containsOnlyTriviaWhiteSpaces)` builds a node of kind
; KindJsxText for BOTH — the distinction moves out of the kind and into this
; bool. So a tree dump sees one kind where the token stream had two, and the
; field is the only place the difference is left.
;
; ★ Stored knowing the yardstick cannot see it (§3.5ao). The reader that would
; make it observable is the TRANSFORM: a whitespace-only child between two tags
; is dropped from a JSX element's children, which is why `<a>\n  <b/>\n</a>` has
; one child and not three. That is neither in this port nor near it.
;
; The text is NOT stored, for the reason LiteralData gives at its own line
; (§3.5q) — every reader of it is a checker or an emitter.
define JsxTextData (contains_only_trivia_white_spaces: bool)

define JsxExpressionData
(
    dot_dot_dot_token: ref[AstNode]?
    expression: ref[AstNode]?
)

define JsxAttributeData
(
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    initializer: ref[AstNode]?
)

define JsxNamespacedNameData
(
    namespace_name: ref[AstNode]
    name: ref[AstNode]?
)


; ── JSDoc (slice 21) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; ★★★ These nodes are NOT in the tree the parser yardstick walks, and that is a
; fact about the REFERENCE rather than about this port: a JSDoc comment hangs
; off the node it documents through a side table (jsdocCache), reached only
; through Node.JSDoc(file) — and for a TS/TSX file it is not even parsed during
; ParseSourceFile, only on the first such access. That is why slice 21 came with
; a THIRD yardstick; see tests/oracle/jsdoc.go for the argument.
;
; ★ Nine tag kinds share ONE record here (§3.5n) because their slot LISTS are
; identical — tag name, one node, comment list — even though the reference names
; the middle slot TypeExpression, ClassName or NameExpression depending on the
; tag. Seven more share the two-slot one. The ARM tells them apart and the kind
; is what the yardstick compares, exactly as WrappedTypeData's arms do.

; The reference's JSDoc{ Comment *NodeList; Tags *NodeList }. `comment` is a
; list of JSDocText and JSDocLink nodes rather than a string, because an inline
; `{@link X}` is a NODE and the prose around it is what is left over.
define JSDocData
(
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    tags: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; JSDocTagBase alone: @public, @private, @protected, @readonly, @override,
; @deprecated and the UNKNOWN tag, which is every tag name the reference does
; not know.
define JSDocTagData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; JSDocTagBase plus ONE node, whatever the reference calls it: @type, @returns,
; @see, @implements, @augments/@extends, @satisfies, @throws, @this, @overload.
define JSDocOneSlotTagData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define JSDocTemplateTagData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    constraint: ref[AstNode]?
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

define JSDocImportTagData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    import_clause: ref[AstNode]?
    module_specifier: ref[AstNode]
    attributes: ref[AstNode]?
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; @typedef and @callback: both carry a type expression AND a full name, in that
; visit order.
define JSDocNamedTagData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    type_expression: ref[AstNode]?
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; The reference's JSDocSignature embeds FunctionLikeBase, so its slots are the
; three a signature has. `type_parameters` is ALWAYS null — NewJSDocSignature is
; called with nil for it at both call sites — and is carried anyway because it
; is a slot of the visit order and leaving it out would make the arm read
; against a different struct than the one it ports.
define JSDocSignatureData
(
    type_parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    parameters: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    type_node: ref[AstNode]?
)

; ★ `is_array_type` is a §3.5ao field: nothing in this port reads it, and the
; reader that would is the checker (getTypeOfJSDocTypeLiteral turns
; `@param {Object[]} o` plus its `@param o.x` children into an ARRAY of an
; anonymous type rather than the type itself). It is stored rather than dropped
; because without it the two shapes are the same node, which is the argument
; ImportTypeData's `is_type_of` already stands on.
define JSDocTypeLiteralData
(
    property_tags: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    is_array_type: bool
)

; ★★★ THE ONE NODE IN THIS PORT WHOSE CHILD ORDER DEPENDS ON A FIELD.
; `@param {T} x` visits the type first, `@param x {T}` visits the name first,
; and `is_name_first` is which — so child_at cannot read its slots off the
; declaration order the way every other arm does. The reference has the same
; shape, in forEachChild_JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag, and it is the reason that
; one node has a hand-written visitor there instead of a generated one.
;
; ★ `is_bracketed` (`@param [x]`, an optional parameter) is a §3.5ao field: its
; reader is the checker.
define JSDocParameterOrPropertyTagData
(
    tag_name: ref[AstNode]
    name: ref[AstNode]?
    is_bracketed: bool
    type_expression: ref[AstNode]?
    is_name_first: bool
    comment: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
)

; ── the CONTROL FLOW GRAPH (slice 92) ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; internal/ast/flow.go, whole. The reference builds this graph in the BIND — a
; flow node on every reachable statement, a label at every junction, an
; antecedent per edge — and reads it in the checker, which is why it sat unported
; through sixty slices: `getFlowTypeOfReference` is the reader, and until slice 92
; nothing here could see whether a graph was right.
;
; ★★★ WHAT MADE IT PORTABLE WAS AN INSTRUMENT, NOT A DECISION. binder.scaly's
; header said the graph is *"not reachable through an exported accessor, so the
; symbols dump cannot see it and neither can any dump this suite has"*, and the
; first half of that is simply false: `Node.FlowNodeData()`, `BodyBase.EndFlowNode`
; and the four `ReturnFlowNode` slots are exported, and an oracle in tests/oracle
; is built INSIDE the reference's module. tests/oracle/flow.go is ten lines of
; walk over those accessors, and with it the construction half is a MEASURED
; slice rather than a prerequisite nothing grades.

define FlowFlagsUnreachable:    int 1 << 0   ; unreachable code
define FlowFlagsStart:          int 1 << 1   ; start of flow graph
define FlowFlagsBranchLabel:    int 1 << 2   ; non-looping junction
define FlowFlagsLoopLabel:      int 1 << 3   ; looping junction
define FlowFlagsAssignment:     int 1 << 4   ; assignment
define FlowFlagsTrueCondition:  int 1 << 5   ; condition known to be true
define FlowFlagsFalseCondition: int 1 << 6   ; condition known to be false
define FlowFlagsSwitchClause:   int 1 << 7   ; switch statement clause
define FlowFlagsArrayMutation:  int 1 << 8   ; potential array mutation
define FlowFlagsCall:           int 1 << 9   ; potential assertion call
define FlowFlagsReduceLabel:    int 1 << 10  ; temporarily reduce antecedents of label
define FlowFlagsReferenced:     int 1 << 11  ; referenced as antecedent once
define FlowFlagsShared:         int 1 << 12  ; referenced as antecedent more than once

define FlowFlagsLabel:     int FlowFlagsBranchLabel | FlowFlagsLoopLabel
define FlowFlagsCondition: int FlowFlagsTrueCondition | FlowFlagsFalseCondition

; ast.FlowSwitchClauseData — the SYNTHETIC node the reference builds so that a
; switch clause's RANGE can ride in the one `node` slot a FlowNode has. It is not
; in the tree: nothing parses it, ForEachChild never reaches it, and the flow dump
; names it by a payload line of its own rather than by kind/pos/end.
;
; ★ It is an arm of NodeData rather than three more fields on FlowNode, because
; that is the reference's own arrangement and because the alternative widens
; EVERY flow node by 24 bytes to serve the one kind that needs them.
define FlowSwitchClauseData
(
    switch_statement: ref[AstNode]?
    clause_start: int
    clause_end: int
)

; ast.FlowReduceLabelData — the second synthetic node, and the payload of the
; reduce label a `try`/`finally` builds: the label whose antecedent list is to be
; temporarily replaced, and the list to replace it with.
define FlowReduceLabelData
(
    target: ref[FlowNode]?
    antecedents: ref[FlowList]?
)

; ast.FlowNode. Four slots, and the two antecedent slots are exclusive by
; construction: a LABEL carries the list, everything else the single antecedent.
; The reference states that in a comment ("Antecedent for all but FlowLabel") and
; enforces it nowhere; this port does the same, because the flow dump compares
; both columns and would say so.
;
; ★ `ast.FlowLabel = ast.FlowNode` upstream is a Go type ALIAS — the same type
; under a second name, for readability at the call sites that require a label.
; Scaly has no alias, and inventing a wrapper concept would make `addAntecedent`
; take something `createBranchLabel` does not return. The name survives in the
; parameter names and in the comments; the type is FlowNode.
define FlowNode
(
    flags: int
    node: ref[AstNode]?
    antecedent: ref[FlowNode]?
    antecedents: ref[FlowList]?
)
{
    function alloc(host: ref[Page]) returns ref[FlowNode]
        host.allocate(sizeof FlowNode, alignof FlowNode) as pointer[FlowNode]
}

; ast.FlowList — a singly linked list of flow nodes, which is what a label's
; antecedents are. The reference allocates these out of an arena of their own and
; SHARES tails between labels (combineFlowLists rebuilds only the head), so a
; list is not a container here either: rebuilding it as an Array would copy a
; structure whose whole point is that it is shared.
define FlowList
(
    flow: ref[FlowNode]?
    next: ref[FlowList]?
)
{
    function alloc(host: ref[Page]) returns ref[FlowList]
        host.allocate(sizeof FlowList, alignof FlowList) as pointer[FlowList]
}

define NodeData union
(
    Token: TokenData
    Identifier: IdentifierData
    StringLiteral: LiteralData
    NumericLiteral: LiteralData
    BigIntLiteral: LiteralData
    NoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral: LiteralData

    ; Parser slice 16. A leaf like its four neighbours: the reference stores the
    ; literal's text and its token flags in the node, and this port stores
    ; neither (§3.5q), so the record is the shared empty one.
    RegularExpressionLiteral: LiteralData

    SourceFile: SourceFileData
    Block: BlockData
    EmptyStatement: EmptyStatementData
    ExpressionStatement: ExpressionStatementData
    VariableStatement: VariableStatementData
    VariableDeclarationList: VariableDeclarationListData
    VariableDeclaration: VariableDeclarationData
    TypeAliasDeclaration: TypeAliasDeclarationData
    TypeParameter: TypeParameterData
    TypeReference: TypeReferenceData
    TypeQuery: TypeQueryData
    QualifiedName: QualifiedNameData
    IndexedAccessType: IndexedAccessTypeData
    ConditionalType: ConditionalTypeData
    TypePredicate: TypePredicateData
    NamedTupleMember: NamedTupleMemberData
    UnionType: TypeListData
    IntersectionType: TypeListData
    TupleType: TypeListData
    ArrayType: WrappedTypeData
    ParenthesizedType: WrappedTypeData
    TypeOperator: TypeOperatorData
    RestType: WrappedTypeData
    OptionalType: WrappedTypeData
    InferType: WrappedTypeData
    LiteralType: WrappedTypeData
    PrefixUnaryExpression: PrefixUnaryExpressionData
    JSDocNullableType: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocNonNullableType: WrappedTypeData
    Parameter: ParameterData
    FunctionDeclaration: FunctionDeclarationData
    FunctionType: FunctionTypeData
    ConstructorType: ConstructorTypeData
    ReturnStatement: ReturnStatementData
    IfStatement: IfStatementData

    ; ── slice 5, appended at the END as the header asks ─────────────────────
    BinaryExpression: BinaryExpressionData
    ConditionalExpression: ConditionalExpressionData
    YieldExpression: YieldExpressionData
    PostfixUnaryExpression: WrappedTypeData
    DeleteExpression: WrappedTypeData
    TypeOfExpression: WrappedTypeData
    VoidExpression: WrappedTypeData
    AwaitExpression: WrappedTypeData
    TypeAssertionExpression: TypeAssertionData
    AsExpression: AsExpressionData
    SatisfiesExpression: AsExpressionData
    NonNullExpression: WrappedTypeData
    PropertyAccessExpression: PropertyAccessData
    ElementAccessExpression: ElementAccessData
    CallExpression: CallExpressionData
    NewExpression: NewExpressionData
    ExpressionWithTypeArguments: ExpressionWithTypeArgumentsData
    MetaProperty: MetaPropertyData
    ParenthesizedExpression: WrappedTypeData
    ArrayLiteralExpression: ElementListData
    ObjectLiteralExpression: ElementListData
    SpreadElement: WrappedTypeData
    SpreadAssignment: WrappedTypeData
    PropertyAssignment: PropertyAssignmentData
    ShorthandPropertyAssignment: ShorthandPropertyAssignmentData
    ComputedPropertyName: WrappedTypeData
    ArrowFunction: ArrowFunctionData
    FunctionExpression: FunctionExpressionData

    ; ── slice 6, appended at the END as the header asks ─────────────────────
    ClassDeclaration: ClassLikeData
    ClassExpression: ClassLikeData
    HeritageClause: HeritageClauseData
    MethodDeclaration: MethodDeclarationData
    PropertyDeclaration: PropertyDeclarationData
    ConstructorDeclaration: ConstructorDeclarationData
    GetAccessorDeclaration: AccessorDeclarationData
    SetAccessorDeclaration: AccessorDeclarationData
    ClassStaticBlockDeclaration: ClassStaticBlockDeclarationData
    IndexSignatureDeclaration: IndexSignatureDeclarationData

    ; `#name`. It has an arm of its own rather than being a Token for the same
    ; reason Identifier does: it carries TEXT in the reference, and the field
    ; this port defers is a field both of them will grow.
    PrivateIdentifier: IdentifierData

    ; ── slice 7, appended at the END as the header asks ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; Two of these reuse a slice-6 record because the reference's slot lists
    ; are IDENTICAL, field for field and in order — not because they are
    ; similar. InterfaceDeclaration is ClassLikeData (modifiers, name,
    ; typeParameters, heritageClauses, members) and PropertySignature is
    ; PropertyDeclarationData (modifiers, name, postfixToken, type,
    ; initializer). Check any future reuse against ast_generated.go the same
    ; way; MethodSignatureData is there because that check FAILED for it.
    TypeLiteral: TypeLiteralData
    CallSignature: SignatureDeclarationData
    ConstructSignature: SignatureDeclarationData
    MethodSignature: MethodSignatureData
    PropertySignature: PropertyDeclarationData
    InterfaceDeclaration: ClassLikeData
    EnumDeclaration: EnumDeclarationData
    EnumMember: EnumMemberData
    ModuleDeclaration: ModuleDeclarationData
    ModuleBlock: BlockData
    MappedType: MappedTypeData

    ; ── slice 8, appended at the END as the header asks ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; Five arms reuse an existing record, each checked against
    ; ast_generated.go the way §3.5n demands rather than by resemblance:
    ; NamespaceImport, NamespaceExport and ExternalModuleReference have
    ; exactly ONE child slot each (WrappedTypeData, the recurring one-child
    ; node — `inner` is the namespace's name and the reference's expression),
    ; and NamedImports and NamedExports have exactly one LIST each
    ; (ElementListData).
    ImportDeclaration: ImportDeclarationData
    ImportEqualsDeclaration: ImportEqualsDeclarationData
    ImportClause: ImportClauseData
    NamespaceImport: WrappedTypeData
    NamedImports: ElementListData
    ImportSpecifier: ImportOrExportSpecifierData
    ExternalModuleReference: WrappedTypeData
    ImportAttributes: ImportAttributesData
    ImportAttribute: ImportAttributeData
    ExportAssignment: ExportAssignmentData
    NamespaceExportDeclaration: NamespaceExportDeclarationData
    ExportDeclaration: ExportDeclarationData
    NamespaceExport: WrappedTypeData
    NamedExports: ElementListData
    ExportSpecifier: ImportOrExportSpecifierData

    ; ── slice 9, appended at the END as the header asks ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; Three arms reuse an existing record, each checked against
    ; ast_generated.go rather than by resemblance:
    ;
    ;   WithStatement    ControlledStatementData — WhileStatement's two slots
    ;                    in WhileStatement's order (see that record's header
    ;                    for why `do` does NOT join them)
    ;   CaseBlock        ElementListData — one list and nothing else, the same
    ;                    reading NamedImports and the array literal are under
    ;   ThrowStatement   ExpressionStatementData — one expression slot that is
    ;                    never null. That is the distinction the
    ;                    ReturnStatementData note draws from the other side:
    ;                    `return;` has no expression, so it needs a record
    ;                    where null MEANS something, while `throw` on its own
    ;                    line gets a zero-width MISSING identifier rather than
    ;                    a null (measured against the reference — the node is
    ;                    there, spanning nothing).
    ;
    ; ★ DebuggerStatement gets NO arm and no record: it carries no field and no
    ; child, so it is a `Token` whose KIND says which it is — exactly the
    ; reading OmittedExpression and SemicolonClassElement are under (see the
    ; header). EmptyStatement, from slice 1, predates that note and keeps its
    ; own arm; the two are not in conflict, one is just older.
    DoStatement: DoStatementData
    WhileStatement: ControlledStatementData
    WithStatement: ControlledStatementData
    ForStatement: ForStatementData
    ForInStatement: ForInOrOfStatementData
    ForOfStatement: ForInOrOfStatementData
    BreakStatement: BreakOrContinueStatementData
    ContinueStatement: BreakOrContinueStatementData
    LabeledStatement: LabeledStatementData
    SwitchStatement: SwitchStatementData
    CaseBlock: ElementListData
    CaseClause: CaseOrDefaultClauseData
    DefaultClause: CaseOrDefaultClauseData
    ThrowStatement: ExpressionStatementData
    TryStatement: TryStatementData
    CatchClause: CatchClauseData

    ; slice 10 — see the two records above for why the patterns share and the
    ; element does not.
    ObjectBindingPattern: ElementListData
    ArrayBindingPattern: ElementListData
    BindingElement: BindingElementData

    ; ── slice 11, appended at the END as the header asks ────────────────────
    ;
    ; The three template-literal-like TOKENS get arms of their own rather than
    ; being `Token`, for the reason Identifier and PrivateIdentifier have theirs:
    ; the reference gives each of them Text, RawText and TemplateFlags, so the
    ; field this port defers is a field all of them will grow. They join
    ; NoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral, which is the fourth member of the
    ; reference's own TemplateLiteralLikeNode group and has been on LiteralData
    ; since slice 1. None of the four has a child.
    TemplateHead: LiteralData
    TemplateMiddle: LiteralData
    TemplateTail: LiteralData

    TemplateExpression: TemplateLiteralData
    TemplateSpan: TemplateSpanData
    TaggedTemplateExpression: TaggedTemplateData
    TemplateLiteralType: TemplateLiteralData
    TemplateLiteralTypeSpan: TemplateSpanData

    ; ── slice 12, appended at the END as the header asks ────────────────────
    ;
    ; ONE arm, and it shares WrappedTypeData for the reason §3.5n gives: the
    ; reference's `Decorator` is `Expression *LeftHandSideExpression` and
    ; nothing else, so the slot list is one slot and the order is trivially the
    ; same. The slot's NAME differs, which is explicitly not part of that test —
    ; the fourteen arms already on this record are called Type, Expression,
    ; Operand and Name.
    ;
    ; A decorator is a MODIFIER as far as every carrier is concerned: it goes
    ; into the same `modifiers` list a `export`/`declare`/`static` keyword goes
    ; into, so no carrying node needed a new slot for this slice and no arm
    ; below changed. That is the reference's own shape (ModifierList holds both,
    ; and `Node.Decorators()` is a FILTER over it), not a simplification.
    Decorator: WrappedTypeData

    ; ── slice 13c, appended at the END as the header asks ───────────────────
    ;
    ; ONE arm, and it is a node this port could not build before it had
    ; diagnostics: MissingDeclaration is what the reference leaves behind when
    ; modifiers or decorators promised a declaration that never arrived —
    ; `@d;`, `export = ;`, a decorated non-class expression. It always follows
    ; a report (Declaration_expected or Expression_expected), which is why it
    ; arrives with them.
    ;
    ; It shares ElementListData for the §3.5n reason: the reference's struct is
    ; StatementBase + DeclarationBase + ModifiersBase, i.e. exactly one slot,
    ; and `ForEachChild` is `visitModifiers(v, node.modifiers)` and nothing
    ; else. One list, one order — the same reading CaseBlock, NamedImports and
    ; the two binding patterns are under. The slot's NAME is `modifiers` rather
    ; than `elements`, which that test explicitly does not look at.
    MissingDeclaration: ElementListData

    ; ── slice 18, appended at the END as the header asks ────────────────────
    ;
    ; ONE arm. It is the last of the reference's type nodes to arrive, and it
    ; could not arrive earlier for the reason the two `import-type` sites said:
    ; it sets a flag on the SOURCE FILE, and until slice 18 nothing carried one.
    ImportType: ImportTypeData

    ; ── slice 20, appended at the END as the header asks ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; The JSX grammar. See the record block above for which arms share and why;
    ; the two FRAGMENT kinds are absent from this list on purpose and are Token.
    JsxElement: JsxElementData
    JsxOpeningElement: JsxOpeningElementData
    JsxSelfClosingElement: JsxOpeningElementData
    JsxClosingElement: WrappedTypeData
    JsxFragment: JsxFragmentData
    JsxText: JsxTextData
    JsxExpression: JsxExpressionData
    JsxAttributes: ElementListData
    JsxAttribute: JsxAttributeData
    JsxSpreadAttribute: WrappedTypeData
    JsxNamespacedName: JsxNamespacedNameData

    ; ── JSDoc (slice 21) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Added at the END, which is the instruction the union's own header gives:
    ; the tag is the jump-table index, so a later regrouping should be one
    ; deliberate move rather than an archaeology exercise. Nothing here is hot —
    ; JSDoc is parsed on demand and walked by one dumper.
    JSDoc: JSDocData
    JSDocText: TokenData
    JSDocTypeExpression: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocNameReference: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocAllType: TokenData
    JSDocNullableType: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocNonNullableType: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocOptionalType: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocVariadicType: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocLink: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocLinkCode: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocLinkPlain: WrappedTypeData
    JSDocUnknownTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocPublicTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocPrivateTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocProtectedTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocReadonlyTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocOverrideTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocDeprecatedTag: JSDocTagData
    JSDocTypeTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocReturnTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocSeeTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocImplementsTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocAugmentsTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocSatisfiesTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocThrowsTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocThisTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocOverloadTag: JSDocOneSlotTagData
    JSDocTemplateTag: JSDocTemplateTagData
    JSDocImportTag: JSDocImportTagData
    JSDocTypedefTag: JSDocNamedTagData
    JSDocCallbackTag: JSDocNamedTagData
    JSDocSignature: JSDocSignatureData
    JSDocTypeLiteral: JSDocTypeLiteralData
    JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTagData

    ; ── the JSDoc REPARSER's two synthesized declarations (slice 22) ─────────
    ;
    ; Both share the record of the node they are the JavaScript spelling of, as
    ; the reference does: NewJSTypeAliasDeclaration fills a TypeAliasDeclaration
    ; and NewJSImportDeclaration an ImportDeclaration; only the KIND differs, and
    ; only the kind has to, because the binder and the checker treat a
    ; JSDoc-declared alias differently from a written one.
    ;
    ; They are arms rather than kinds on the existing arm because `choose` reads
    ; the ARM: a shared record buys a shared BODY and never a shared arm, and a
    ; missing arm is a node with no children and no diagnostic (§3.5bb).
    JSTypeAliasDeclaration: TypeAliasDeclarationData
    JSImportDeclaration: ImportDeclarationData

    ; ── the two SYNTHETIC arms of the flow graph (slice 92) ─────────────────
    ;
    ; Neither is parsed and neither is walked: the binder makes them so that a
    ; switch clause's range and a reduce label's antecedent list can ride in the
    ; one `node` slot a FlowNode has, exactly as the reference does with
    ; NewFlowSwitchClauseData and NewFlowReduceLabelData (both KindUnknown).
    ;
    ; ★ They need no ForEachChild arm and get none. §3.5bb's rule — a missing arm
    ; is a node with no children and no diagnostic — is the hazard for a node the
    ; PARSER produces; for one the parser cannot produce it is the specification.
    FlowSwitchClause: FlowSwitchClauseData
    FlowReduceLabel: FlowReduceLabelData
)

; ── ast.JSDeclarationKind (slice 33) ────────────────────────────────────────
;
; What GetAssignmentDeclarationKind answers: which of the five JavaScript
; assignment DECLARATIONS an assignment or an `Object.defineProperty` call is,
; and `None` for everything else. The numbers are upstream's iota order and
; nothing compares them for magnitude, but they travel as the `unported` DETAIL
; of the arm that reports them, so a renumbering would rewrite a histogram.
;
; ★ They live here rather than beside the classifier in parser.scaly because
; this is where the reference keeps them (internal/ast/utilities.go) and because
; BOTH later modules read them — the JSDoc reparser asks the classifier for a
; boolean, the binder for the kind — and a module-level constant is visible only
; from a module declared after the one that defines it.
define JSDeclarationKindNone: int 0

; module.exports = expr, except for module.exports = exports
define JSDeclarationKindModuleExports: int 1

; exports.name = expr / module.exports.name = expr
define JSDeclarationKindExportsProperty: int 2

; this.name = expr
define JSDeclarationKindThisProperty: int 3

; F.name = expr, F[name] = expr — in a JS file OR a TS one, which is the arm
; that makes this classifier the binder's business rather than the reparser's.
define JSDeclarationKindProperty: int 4

; Object.defineProperty(x, "name", { ... })
define JSDeclarationKindObjectDefinePropertyValue: int 5

; Object.defineProperty(exports || module.exports, "name", { ... })
define JSDeclarationKindObjectDefinePropertyExports: int 6

define AstNode
(
    kind: int
    flags: int

    ; The reference's core.TextRange, spelled out. `pos` is the FULL start —
    ; leading trivia included — and `end` is where the token ended. Keeping them
    ; two ints rather than a nested by-value struct is deliberate: a by-value
    ; struct nested in a struct is not laid out in a root that never declared it
    ; (exit 16), and this type is read from a program root.
    pos: int
    end: int

    data: NodeData

    ; ── the four slots the BINDER needs (slices 27 and 65) ───────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ DIVERGENCE FROM THE REFERENCE'S PLACEMENT, and it is a consequence of
    ; §3.1's sum type. Upstream puts these in the per-kind data structs —
    ; `DeclarationBase.Symbol`, `LocalsContainerBase.locals`, `NodeBase.Parent` —
    ; and asks `node.Symbol()` / `node.Locals()`, which answer nil for a kind whose
    ; data embeds neither base. Here the payload is a union of 134 records, so the
    ; same arrangement would mean adding a field to every record that can carry a
    ; symbol (44 of them, measured over the corpus) and a second one to every
    ; container kind (25). They sit on the NODE instead: FOUR pointers, 32 bytes on
    ; every node since slice 65 gave the local symbol its slot, region-allocated and
    ; freed with the parse.
    ;
    ; ★ The observable behaviour is the same because the BINDER decides what gets
    ; one: a kind the reference gives no symbol to is a kind nothing here assigns
    ; to, and the symbols dump compares exactly that. What is lost is the
    ; compiler's refusal — upstream cannot even write `someToken.Symbol = x`,
    ; while here it type-checks. The yardstick is the check.
    ;
    ; `parent` is the reference's Node.Parent and is set during the PARSE, by
    ; finish_node, from the node that owns the child (parser.go's
    ; overrideParentInImmediateChildren). It is not derivable in general — the
    ; reference re-parents at four sites where the owner was never finished — even
    ; though over the whole stage-2 corpus it agrees with the walk everywhere; the
    ; header note above has the measurement. Its readers are GetContainerFlags
    ; (a Block's flags depend on whether its parent is function-like),
    ; bindParameter, bindTypeParameter and getDeclarationName.
    parent: ref[AstNode]?

    ; The reference's DeclarationBase.Symbol: the symbol this declaration node
    ; owns, set by addDeclarationToSymbol. Null on every node the binder does not
    ; declare, which is most of them.
    symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; The reference's ExportableBase.LocalSymbol, written by declareModuleMember's
    ; exported arm: an EXPORTED declaration owns two symbols, and this is the LOCAL
    ; one, the export being what `symbol` holds.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 65 IS THE READER THAT EARNS IT, and the slot had been refused with
    ; a reason that named a DIFFERENT reader. The note that stood at
    ; declare_module_member said the only reader in the whole reference is
    ; nameresolver.go's GetLocalSymbolForExportDefault — which is true of
    ; internal/binder and false of the reference: checkExportsOnMergedDeclarations
    ; opens with `symbol := node.LocalSymbol()`, and checker.go:3413 reads it too.
    ; A slot refused for want of a reader is §3.5be's rule working; a slot refused
    ; on a SURVEY of one package is the same shape as §3.5cp's expired deferral,
    ; because nothing re-reads the survey when the port crosses into the package it
    ; did not cover.
    local_symbol: ref[Symbol]?

    ; The reference's LocalsContainerBase.locals, created LAZILY: upstream's
    ; bindContainer has the eager creation commented out and every reader goes
    ; through ast.GetLocals, which makes the table on first use. That laziness is
    ; observable — 754 of the 12 444 stage-2 files have no locals table at all —
    ; so it is ported as laziness rather than as a table per container.
    locals: ref[SymbolTable]?

    ; ── the four FLOW slots (slice 92) ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SAME DIVERGENCE AS THE FOUR ABOVE, AND IT IS NOT FREE: eight
    ; pointers on every node now, 64 bytes. Upstream puts these in the per-kind
    ; data structs — `FlowNodeBase.FlowNode` reaches 46 kinds, `BodyBase.EndFlow
    ; Node` eight plus the source file, `ReturnFlowNode` four and
    ; `CaseOrDefaultClause.FallthroughFlowNode` two — and asks
    ; `node.FlowNodeData()`, which answers nil for a kind whose data embeds none
    ; of them. Here the payload is a union, so the same arrangement means a field
    ; on every record that could carry one, at eleven records and every one of
    ; their construction sites.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE `FlowNodeData() != nil` GUARD IS VACUOUS HERE — MEASURED, NOT
    ; ASSUMED, AND IT IS WHY THIS SLICE BUILDS NO 46-KIND TABLE. The guard stands
    ; at five sites upstream and each one is already gated on a kind that has the
    ; slot: bindChildren's statement line asks `KindFirstStatement <= k <=
    ; KindLastStatement`, and all seventeen of those embed StatementBase;
    ; `setFlowNode` is called for a property access, an element access, a function
    ; expression or arrow, and an object-literal/class-expression method, and all
    ; of those have one; and the UNREACHABLE arm writes NIL, which on a node
    ; upstream gives no slot to is a write of null over null. So the SET of nodes
    ; carrying a non-nil flow node is the same on both sides without a predicate —
    ; and the flow dump compares exactly that set, which is what makes this a
    ; measurement rather than a reading. ★§3.5be forbids machinery no instrument
    ; can reach; a table whose every row is unreachable is that machinery, spelled
    ; as data.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE SET IS WHAT FOUND THE TWO WRITES NO READING OF THE SWITCH
    ; WOULD: `bindFunctionExpression` and `bindPropertyOrMethodOrAccessor` write
    ; the slot from inside a DECLARATION arm, nowhere near the five that look like
    ; flow. 91 of 1 453 units disagreed on the first run and named the kinds —
    ; FunctionExpression and ArrowFunction — in the first line of the diff.
    flow_node: ref[FlowNode]?

    ; BodyBase.EndFlowNode and SourceFile.EndFlowNode — the flow node the walk
    ; ended on, written by bindContainer for a function-like with a present body
    ; and for the source file. Its reader is the checker's return-path analysis;
    ; the flow dump is what measures it in this slice.
    end_flow_node: ref[FlowNode]?

    ; ReturnFlowNode, on four kinds upstream and written by bindContainer for
    ; exactly TWO of them — a constructor and a class static block. The other two
    ; arms of setReturnFlowNode (a function declaration, a function expression)
    ; have no caller in the reference at this pin; they are ported as the switch
    ; the reference writes, and the flow dump prints a column that is -1 for both
    ; on every unit of both corpora.
    return_flow_node: ref[FlowNode]?

    ; CaseOrDefaultClause.FallthroughFlowNode — the flow a clause falls THROUGH
    ; to its successor with, written by bindCaseBlock. Upstream keeps it on the
    ; clause record rather than in FlowNodeBase, because it is not the flow the
    ; clause is REACHED by; the checker's switch narrowing reads it.
    fallthrough_flow_node: ref[FlowNode]?
)
{
    function alloc(host: ref[Page]) returns ref[AstNode]
        host.allocate(sizeof AstNode, alignof AstNode) as pointer[AstNode]

    ; The reference's NodeList. It carries its own TextRange, which this port
    ; does not keep yet — nothing reads a list's extent, and the AST yardstick
    ; cannot see one either (ForEachChild visits a list's ELEMENTS, never the
    ; list). Stated because a hidden distinction hides every bug in it.
    function new_node_list(host: ref[Page]) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
    {
        let a host.allocate(sizeof Array[ref[AstNode]?], alignof Array[ref[AstNode]?]) as pointer[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]
        set *a: Array[ref[AstNode]?]()
        a
    }

    ; ── accessors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Statics taking the node by pointer, not `this` methods: a user program
    ; calling a CROSS-PACKAGE free function with a concept-pointer parameter
    ; fails resolution, while a concept STATIC resolves (root CLAUDE.md). The
    ; dumper is such a program.

    ; ★NULLABLE, and KindUnknown is the answer -- binary_operator_kind# three
    ; hundred lines down already reads that way, and it is what the reference
    ; means: Go writes `n != nil && n.Kind == K`, so `kind_of(null) = K` must
    ; be FALSE and `<> K` TRUE, which KindUnknown gives on both sides. An
    ; absent token is not a readonly keyword; it is not a wrong kind either.
    function kind_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        n.kind
    }

    ; ── the binder's three slots, read back ─────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `parent_node_of` rather than `parent_of`, because Symbol has a parent too and
    ; the two questions read very differently at a call site: a node's parent is a
    ; NODE and a symbol's is a SYMBOL.

    function parent_node_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.parent
    }

    function symbol_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.symbol
    }

    ; Node.LocalSymbol. Null-safe for the reason symbol_of is: upstream's reads the
    ; ExportableData, which answers nil for a kind that has none, so a null node
    ; and a node with no slot must give the same answer.
    function local_symbol_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Symbol]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.local_symbol
    }

    function locals_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[SymbolTable]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.locals
    }

    ; Node.StatementList/Statements — the FOUR kinds that have one: the source
    ; file, a block, a module block and a case or default clause. The binder needs
    ; it as a list rather than through child_at, because
    ; bindEachStatementFunctionsFirst walks it TWICE.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT HAD THREE OF THE FOUR UNTIL SLICE 64, and the missing one is §3.5bk
    ; exactly: the reference PANICS on a kind its switch does not name while this
    ; answers null, so a clause asked for its statements would have come back as an
    ; EMPTY LIST — a well-formed wrong answer, and the loop over it would simply not
    ; run. The binder never asked (it reaches a clause's statements through the
    ; child walk), so the narrowing cost nothing until checkSwitchStatement, whose
    ; per-clause `c.checkSourceElements(clause.Statements())` is the whole reason
    ; the statements inside a `switch` are checked at all.
    function statements_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
                return f.statements
            when b: Block
                return b.statements
            when mb: ModuleBlock
                return mb.statements
            when cc: CaseClause
                return cc.statements
            when dc: DefaultClause
                return dc.statements
        null
    }

    ; ── slice 64: the two slots of the `switch` head ─────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference has no generic accessor for either — checkSwitchStatement
    ; reaches them as `node.AsSwitchStatement().CaseBlock` and
    ; `caseBlock.AsCaseBlock().Clauses.Nodes` — so §3.5bk has nothing to widen to
    ; and the breadth is the RECORD's: one arm each, exactly as the three `for`-head
    ; slots and the six `if`/`try` slots below are one arm each.
    ;
    ; ★ `case_clauses_of` is NOT an arm of `elements_of`, even though CaseBlock
    ; shares ElementListData with the six kinds that accessor serves. The reference's
    ; Node.ElementList() names five kinds and CaseBlock is not among them, so adding
    ; the arm there would widen a port accessor PAST the reference function it is a
    ; port of — which is the mirror image of the narrowing §3.5bk is about, and just
    ; as invisible.
    function case_block_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ss: SwitchStatement
                return ss.case_block
        null
    }

    function case_clauses_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when cb: CaseBlock
                return cb.elements
        null
    }

    function end_of_file_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
                return f.end_of_file_token
        null
    }

    ; The PROPERTY name of a binding element or an import/export specifier — the
    ; `p` of `{ p: x }` and of `import { p as x }`. IsIdentifierName asks for it,
    ; and answering null there would be a silent wrong answer rather than a gap.
    function property_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when be: BindingElement
                return be.property_name
            when isp: ImportSpecifier
                return isp.property_name
            when esp: ExportSpecifier
                return esp.property_name
        null
    }

    ; The `*` of a generator, and of a `yield*`. GetFunctionFlags reads it off
    ; BodyData, which the YieldExpression does NOT have — so the fourth arm here
    ; is wider than that one caller, in the shape dot_dot_dot_token_of below is
    ; wider than the binder's two: the accessor answers for every kind that
    ; CARRIES the field and each caller asks only where the reference asks.
    function asterisk_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.asterisk_token
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.asterisk_token
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.asterisk_token
            when ye: YieldExpression
                return ye.asterisk_token
        null
    }

    ; ast.IsBindingPattern — a two-kind test, and it is here rather than spelled
    ; out at its caller because the reference has it as a named predicate and
    ; because the two kinds are the sort of pair a second reader gets wrong.
    function is_binding_pattern(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k kind_of(n)
        if k = KindObjectBindingPattern
            return true
        if k = KindArrayBindingPattern
            return true
        false
    }

    ; The `...` token of a parameter or a binding element. Both flow arms bind it
    ; FIRST, before the name, so the binder asks for it by itself rather than
    ; through the child order.
    function dot_dot_dot_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when p: Parameter
                return p.dot_dot_dot_token
            when be: BindingElement
                return be.dot_dot_dot_token

            ; ★ Slice 61. A NAMED TUPLE MEMBER carries one too — `[...rest: T[]]` —
            ; and getTupleElementFlags and checkNamedTupleMember both read it. The
            ; arm is safe to add because every caller of this function establishes
            ; the kind first (the two binder arms and the five checker ones are all
            ; inside a kind test), so widening cannot change an existing answer;
            ; question_token_of has carried the same arm since the member landed.
            when ntm: NamedTupleMember
                return ntm.dot_dot_dot_token
        null
    }

    function flags_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
        n.flags

    ; The SourceFile's diagnostic list, read through the node exactly as its
    ; children are — see SourceFileData. Answers 0 / false for any other kind,
    ; which is what the dump wants: only a SourceFile has them.
    function diagnostic_count(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
            {
                if f.diagnostics = null
                    return 0
                return f.diagnostics.length()
            }
        0
    }

    function diagnostic_at(n: ref[AstNode]?, i: int, out_pos: ref[int], out_end: ref[int], out_code: ref[int]) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
            {
                if f.diagnostics = null
                    return false
                return f.diagnostics.at(i, out_pos, out_end, out_code)
            }
        false
    }

    ; The JS-syntax list, read the same way — see SourceFileData. Null for every
    ; file that is not JavaScript, which answers 0 here.
    function js_diagnostic_count(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
            {
                if f.js_diagnostics = null
                    return 0
                return f.js_diagnostics.length()
            }
        0
    }

    function js_diagnostic_at(n: ref[AstNode]?, i: int, out_pos: ref[int], out_end: ref[int], out_code: ref[int]) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
            {
                if f.js_diagnostics = null
                    return false
                return f.js_diagnostics.at(i, out_pos, out_end, out_code)
            }
        false
    }

    ; ── the list-range side table, read from either phase ────────────────────
    ;
    ; The SEARCH, moved off the Parser when the table gained a second reader (see
    ; ListRange). Searched from the most recent end, because the caller that owns
    ; the table asks about a list it has just built; the Checker's lookups do not
    ; have that locality and do not need it — the table has one row per delimited
    ; list of the file.
    ;
    ; ★ Answering FALSE is not an error and the two readers say why differently.
    ; In the parser it means the JSDoc reparser synthesized the list whole, and
    ; every reader there first asks whether any element is un-reparsed, which such
    ; a list has none of. In the checker it means the same thing arriving from the
    ; other side, and there it is REPORTED rather than guessed: a span invented
    ; here is a wrong answer that looks like a right one, and the fifth yardstick
    ; compares spans.
    function find_list_range(table: ref[Array[ListRange]]?, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, out_pos: ref[int], out_end: ref[int]) returns bool
    {
        if table = null
            return false
        if list = null
            return false
        var i table.get_length() as int
        while i > 0
        {
            set i: i - 1
            let r table[i]
            if r.list = list
            {
                set out_pos: r.pos
                set out_end: r.end
                return true
            }
        }
        false
    }

    ; The same question asked of the FILE, which is the form the checker has: it
    ; holds the SourceFile node and no parser. Answers false for any other kind,
    ; the way diagnostic_count does.
    function list_range_of(n: ref[AstNode]?, list: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, out_pos: ref[int], out_end: ref[int]) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when f: SourceFile
                return AstNode.find_list_range(f.list_ranges, list, out_pos, out_end)
        false
    }

    function pos_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
        n.pos

    function end_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns int
        n.end

    ; ── two payload questions the parser has to ask ─────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Both exist because the payload is a UNION and `choose` lives here, with
    ; the arms. The alternative — reaching into the payload from parser.scaly —
    ; would put a second copy of the arm list in a file that has no reason to
    ; know one.

    ; The operator of a BinaryExpression, or KindUnknown for anything else.
    ; parseBinaryExpressionRest needs it to decide how far an `as` may reach.
    function binary_operator_kind(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return kind_of(be.operator_token)
        KindUnknown
    }

    ; ast.IsSignedNumericLiteral: `+1` or `-1`, i.e. a prefix unary expression
    ; whose operator is a plus or a minus and whose operand is a numeric literal.
    ; Two readers, and both are about a COMPUTED property name: HasDynamicName
    ; asks whether `[-1]: T` needs an anonymous symbol (it does not — a signed
    ; literal is a static name), and getDeclarationName spells that name back out.
    ; The second half needs the operator's TOKEN TEXT and this port stores no
    ; literal text (§3.5bc), so binder.scaly reports there; this predicate is the
    ; half that can be answered.
    ;
    ; It lives here, with the arms, for the reason binary_operator_kind gives: the
    ; payload is a union and reaching into it from another file would put a second
    ; copy of the arm list where nothing else needs one.
    ; ── the flow slots' accessors (slice 92) ─────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Four reads and five payload reads, and every one of them has exactly ONE
    ; caller: FlowDump. The binder writes the slots through `this` and never needs
    ; a getter; the dump takes a `ref[AstNode]?` off the walk and does.
    function flow_node_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.flow_node
    }

    function end_flow_node_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.end_flow_node
    }

    ; UnionTypeNode.Types / IntersectionTypeNode.Types / TupleTypeNode.Elements —
    ; the three kinds whose payload is a bare TypeListData, slice 94. One accessor
    ; because it is one question and one record; the CALLER knows which kind it
    ; asked about, exactly as target_of does for the two object arms.
    function type_list_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        choose n.data
            when ut: UnionType
                return ut.types
            when it: IntersectionType
                return it.types
            when tt: TupleType
                return tt.types
        null
    }

    function return_flow_node_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.return_flow_node
    }

    function fallthrough_flow_node_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        n.fallthrough_flow_node
    }

    function flow_switch_statement_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when d: FlowSwitchClause
                return d.switch_statement
        null
    }

    function flow_clause_start_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when d: FlowSwitchClause
                return d.clause_start
        0
    }

    function flow_clause_end_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when d: FlowSwitchClause
                return d.clause_end
        0
    }

    function flow_reduce_target_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when d: FlowReduceLabel
                return d.target
        null
    }

    function flow_reduce_antecedents_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[FlowList]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when d: FlowReduceLabel
                return d.antecedents
        null
    }

    function get_immediately_invoked_function_expression(fn: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        let k AstNode.kind_of(fn)
        var eligible false
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            set eligible: true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            set eligible: true
        if eligible = false
            return null
        var prev fn
        var parent AstNode.parent_node_of(fn)
        while parent <> null
        {
            if AstNode.kind_of(parent as ref[AstNode]) <> KindParenthesizedExpression
                break
            set prev: parent as ref[AstNode]
            set parent: AstNode.parent_node_of(parent)
        }
        if parent = null
            return null
        let p parent as ref[AstNode]
        if AstNode.kind_of(p) <> KindCallExpression
            return null
        if AstNode.expression_of(p) <> prev
            return null
        p
    }

    function is_signed_numeric_literal(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when pu: PrefixUnaryExpression
            {
                var signed false
                if pu.operator_kind = KindPlusToken
                    set signed: true
                if pu.operator_kind = KindMinusToken
                    set signed: true
                if signed = false
                    return false
                return kind_of(pu.operand) = KindNumericLiteral
            }
        false
    }

    ; The type argument LIST of an ExpressionWithTypeArguments — the one child
    ; slot that is a list and that a caller has to move rather than walk, when
    ; `f<T>(x)` is folded back from two nodes into one CallExpression.
    ; Node.TypeArgumentList(), whole. ★ The reference PANICS on a kind it does not
    ; list, so its switch is the exhaustive one and this had one arm of nine:
    ; checkJSSyntax asks it of a call, a `new`, a tagged template and the two JSX
    ; element forms. Widening is safe for the four existing readers because every
    ; one of them is already guarded by `kind = KindExpressionWithTypeArguments`.
    function type_arguments_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when e: ExpressionWithTypeArguments
                return e.type_arguments
            when tref: TypeReference
                return tref.type_arguments
            when tqry: TypeQuery
                return tqry.type_arguments
            when calx: CallExpression
                return calx.type_arguments
            when newx: NewExpression
                return newx.type_arguments
            when tagt: TaggedTemplateExpression
                return tagt.type_arguments
            when impt: ImportType
                return impt.type_arguments
            when jsxo: JsxOpeningElement
                return jsxo.type_arguments
            when jsxs: JsxSelfClosingElement
                return jsxs.type_arguments
        null
    }

    ; ImportTypeData.is_type_of — `typeof import("m")` against `import("m")`.
    ;
    ; ★★★ SLICE 47 IS THE READER THE FIELD'S OWN NOTE PREDICTED. It has been stored
    ; since the type-node slice with §3.5ao's report over it — *the dump compares
    ; kind, range and flags, so a wrong value here would pass; the reader that
    ; would make it observable is the CHECKER* — and this is that reader arriving:
    ; is_part_of_type_node_in_parent answers `!parent.IsTypeOf` for a node under an
    ; ImportType, which decides whether that node gets a T line at all. So the
    ; field is now observable through the fifth yardstick, in the direction the
    ; note named.
    function import_type_is_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when impt: ImportType
                return impt.is_type_of
        false
    }

    ; TypeParameterDeclaration.Constraint, asked by is_part_of_type_node_in_parent:
    ; a node under a type parameter is part of a type node exactly when it IS the
    ; constraint (the DEFAULT is a type too, but the reference tests only the
    ; constraint here, and a default's own kind lands it in the FirstTypeNode range
    ; instead).
    function type_parameter_constraint_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when tp: TypeParameter
                return tp.constraint
        null
    }

    ; ── ast.IsDeclarationNode / ast.IsDeclaration ───────────────────────────
    ;
    ; `IsDeclarationNode(node)` is `node.DeclarationData() != nil` — a question
    ; about the node's LAYOUT and not about its kind, which is why the answer is
    ; DERIVED rather than written: DeclarationKinds.scaly is generated by closing
    ; the reference's own struct-embedding graph over `DeclarationBase`, and
    ; tools/gendeclarationkinds.py's header has the whole argument, including the
    ; seven kinds no reader would have guessed and the proof that the pairing
    ; kind→struct is single-valued (which is what makes the reduction to a kind
    ; legitimate at all).
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 47 deferred `check_grammar_source_file`'s second half on exactly
    ; this predicate, in writing — *a missing kind drops a TS1046 the reference
    ; reports, an extra one invents a diagnostic where a plain statement stands* —
    ; so this is that bill paid, and the checker's arm is its first reader.
    function is_declaration_node(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        is_declaration_node_kind(kind_of(n))
    }

    ; ast.IsDeclaration. The reference's one departure from IsDeclarationNode: a
    ; TypeParameter counts only once it has a parent, because the parser builds a
    ; free-standing one while re-parsing a JSDoc signature and `IsDeclaration` is
    ; asked of nodes in both states.
    function is_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if kind_of(n) = KindTypeParameter
            return parent_node_of(n) <> null
        is_declaration_node(n)
    }

    ; ── ast.IsPartOfTypeNode and its two helpers ────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHY THIS FAMILY IS PORTED IN A SLICE THAT CHECKS ALMOST NOTHING: it is
    ; the fifth yardstick's own CONTRACT rather than a convenience. The reference's
    ; baseline type walker may not ask a type node for its type — GetTypeAtLocation
    ; nil-dereferences inside the checker — so the walk's exclusion list is what
    ; keeps the oracle alive, and OUR side has to exclude exactly the same nodes or
    ; the T section differs as a shifted list of positions with no clue which test
    ; moved. It is also the fourth arm of getTypeOfNode, so the checker asks it
    ; too.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's own comment on the recursion, kept because it is the whole
    ; reason the helper exists: in `let a: A.B.C`, calling IsPartOfTypeNode on the
    ; parent would make the qualified name `A.B` a type node — only `C` and `A.B.C`
    ; are. So the helper does NOT recurse; it asks about the parent's kind and
    ; stops.
    function is_part_of_type_node(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k kind_of(n)
        if k >= KindFirstTypeNode
        {
            if k <= KindLastTypeNode
                return true
        }

        ; The eleven keyword types. `void` is the twelfth and is context-dependent,
        ; because `void 0` is an expression.
        if k = KindAnyKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindUnknownKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindNumberKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindBigIntKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindStringKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindBooleanKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindSymbolKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindObjectKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindUndefinedKeyword
            return true
        ; ★ KindNullKeyword is in this list, and it is the arm that explains a dump:
        ; the reference answers NO type line for the `null` in `null;` — the literal
        ; is excluded from the type walk as though it were a type annotation,
        ; because in a LiteralType position it is one and the predicate does not
        ; distinguish. Measured against the oracle rather than read: a one-line
        ; `null` unit produces the ExpressionStatement and the EOF token only.
        if k = KindNullKeyword
            return true
        if k = KindNeverKeyword
            return true

        if k = KindVoidKeyword
        {
            let p parent_node_of(n)
            if p = null
                return true
            return kind_of(p) <> KindVoidExpression
        }

        if k = KindExpressionWithTypeArguments
            return is_part_of_type_expression_with_type_arguments(n)

        if k = KindTypeParameter
        {
            let p parent_node_of(n)
            if p = null
                return false
            if kind_of(p) = KindMappedType
                return true
            return kind_of(p) = KindInferType
        }

        if k = KindIdentifier
        {
            let p parent_node_of(n)
            if p = null
                return false
            ; The right-hand side of a qualified name or a property access is
            ; asked ABOUT ITS PARENT, because `A.B.C`'s `C` is part of a type node
            ; exactly when `A.B.C` is.
            if kind_of(p) = KindQualifiedName
            {
                if qualified_right_of(p) = n
                    return is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(p)
            }
            if kind_of(p) = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            {
                if property_access_name_of(p) = n
                    return is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(p)
            }
            return is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(n)
        }

        if k = KindQualifiedName
            return is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(n)
        if k = KindPropertyAccessExpression
            return is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(n)
        if k = KindThisKeyword
            return is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(n)

        false
    }

    function is_part_of_type_node_in_parent(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let p parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        let pk kind_of(p)

        if pk = KindTypeQuery
            return false
        ; `typeof import("m")` — the ARGUMENT of a `typeof` import is not part of a
        ; type node, while the argument of a plain `import("m")` type is. This is
        ; the field's first reader; see import_type_is_type_of.
        if pk = KindImportType
            return import_type_is_type_of(p) = false

        ; See the header: NOT a recursive call. The parent's own kind decides.
        if pk >= KindFirstTypeNode
        {
            if pk <= KindLastTypeNode
                return true
        }

        if pk = KindExpressionWithTypeArguments
            return is_part_of_type_expression_with_type_arguments(p)
        if pk = KindTypeParameter
            return type_parameter_constraint_of(p) = n

        ; The sixteen kinds with a TYPE slot: a node under one of them is part of a
        ; type node exactly when it IS that slot.
        if is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node(pk)
            return type_of(p) = n

        ; A type ARGUMENT of a call, a `new` or a tagged template.
        if pk = KindCallExpression
            return list_contains(type_arguments_of(p), n)
        if pk = KindNewExpression
            return list_contains(type_arguments_of(p), n)
        if pk = KindTaggedTemplateExpression
            return list_contains(type_arguments_of(p), n)

        false
    }

    ; The reference writes this list inline in one `case`; it is a function here for
    ; the reason every long kind list in this port is one — a `case` with sixteen
    ; labels is a line nobody re-reads, and the arm above has to stay one
    ; statement.
    function is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node(pk: int) returns bool
    {
        if pk = KindVariableDeclaration
            return true
        if pk = KindParameter
            return true
        if pk = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if pk = KindPropertySignature
            return true
        if pk = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if pk = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if pk = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        if pk = KindConstructor
            return true
        if pk = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if pk = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if pk = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if pk = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if pk = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if pk = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        if pk = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if pk = KindTypeAssertionExpression
            return true
        false
    }

    ; isPartOfTypeExpressionWithTypeArguments: an ExpressionWithTypeArguments is a
    ; type node EXCEPT in a class's `extends` clause, where it is an expression —
    ; which is the same distinction the type walk's class-extends workaround exists
    ; for (a `class D extends C` must report `C`, not `typeof C`).
    function is_part_of_type_expression_with_type_arguments(n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        let p parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        let pk kind_of(p)
        if pk = KindJSDocImplementsTag
            return true
        if pk = KindJSDocAugmentsTag
            return true
        if pk <> KindHeritageClause
            return false
        let gp parent_node_of(p)
        if gp = null
            return true
        let gk kind_of(gp)
        ; !IsClassLike(parent.Parent) || parent.Token == KindImplementsKeyword
        if gk = KindClassDeclaration
            return heritage_clause_token_of(p) = KindImplementsKeyword
        if gk = KindClassExpression
            return heritage_clause_token_of(p) = KindImplementsKeyword
        true
    }

    ; ast.IsTypeOrJSTypeAliasDeclaration, whole. Its reader is the type walk's
    ; identifier exclusion: a type alias's NAME is the one declaration name that is
    ; asked for its type even though it declares nothing into the value space.
    function is_type_or_js_type_alias_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k kind_of(n)
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        k = KindJSTypeAliasDeclaration
    }

    ; ast.IsExpressionWithTypeArgumentsInClassExtendsClause, which is
    ; TryGetClassExtendingExpressionWithTypeArguments != nil, which is
    ; TryGetClassImplementingOrExtendingExpressionWithTypeArguments with the
    ; implements half rejected. Flattened to the one question both readers ask, and
    ; the flattening is safe because neither of them wants the CLASS: the type
    ; walk's base-class workaround asks the parent for its type, and
    ; getTypeOfNode's own class arms are not ported.
    ;
    ; ★ When they are, this must split back into the pair — the class node AND the
    ; isImplements flag — because getTypeOfNode reads both (`classType != nil &&
    ; !isImplements` is one of its arms). Said here so the split is a widening
    ; rather than a rediscovery.
    function is_expression_with_type_arguments_in_class_extends_clause(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if kind_of(n) <> KindExpressionWithTypeArguments
            return false
        let p parent_node_of(n)
        if p = null
            return false
        if kind_of(p) <> KindHeritageClause
            return false
        let gp parent_node_of(p)
        if gp = null
            return false
        var class_like false
        if kind_of(gp) = KindClassDeclaration
            set class_like: true
        if kind_of(gp) = KindClassExpression
            set class_like: true
        if class_like = false
            return false
        heritage_clause_token_of(p) <> KindImplementsKeyword
    }

    ; slices.Contains over a node list. One caller family (the three type-argument
    ; arms above), and it is a function rather than three loops for the reason
    ; given at is_kind_with_type_slot_for_part_of_type_node.
    function list_contains(a: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, n: ref[AstNode]) returns bool
    {
        if a = null
            return false
        var i 0
        while i < list_count(a)
        {
            if child_in_list(a, i) = n
                return true
            set i: i + 1
        }
        false
    }
    ; The LITERAL slot of a template span, which is what ends the span loop:
    ; parseTemplateSpans keeps going while `span.Literal.Kind == TemplateMiddle`
    ; and stops on a TemplateTail. It is an accessor rather than child_at(n, 1)
    ; because that index is only the literal while the expression slot is
    ; non-null — child_at SKIPS nulls, so a degenerate span would silently
    ; answer the literal at index 0 and the loop would read its own tail as an
    ; expression.
    function template_span_literal(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ts: TemplateSpan
                return ts.literal
            when tlts: TemplateLiteralTypeSpan
                return tlts.literal
        null
    }

    ; The kind of an ImportEqualsDeclaration's MODULE REFERENCE, or
    ; KindUnknown for any other node. `isAnExternalModuleIndicatorNode` asks
    ; exactly this — an `import x = require("m")` makes the file a module while
    ; an `import x = A.B` does not — and asking it by child INDEX would be
    ; wrong the moment the declaration carries a modifier.
    function import_equals_reference_kind(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when ie: ImportEqualsDeclaration
                return kind_of(ie.module_reference)
        KindUnknown
    }

    ; ── JSDoc accessors (slice 21) ──────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Here rather than in parser.scaly for the reason binary_operator_kind gives:
    ; a `choose` over the payload belongs in the file that declares the payload.

    ; The reference's `node.Text() == "..."` on a tag NAME, which is how
    ; parseTag and tryParseChildTag dispatch. A C literal rather than a String
    ; because every caller is a literal and the identifier's text is a raw
    ; pointer and length.
    ; ★★★ THE PrivateIdentifier ARM IS SLICE 35's, and it is §3.5bb caught in the
    ; act for the fourth time: PrivateIdentifier and Identifier SHARE
    ; IdentifierData, so the record has the text either way — and a `when` arm
    ; names the TAG, so a private name answered null here and length 0 below.
    ; checkPrivateIdentifier then compared `#constructor` against nothing and
    ; never reported TS18012, with rc 0 and no marker; the reference's `Node.Text()`
    ; is ONE function over both kinds. Found by the fixture written to gate that
    ; check, on the run that was supposed to confirm it — which is the argument for
    ; writing the fixture rather than reading the arm.
    ; ★★★ SLICE 38 PAID FOR THIS CLASS A SECOND TIME, and the paragraph above is
    ; the receipt for the first: the answer then was to add ONE more payload arm to
    ; a function named after the FIRST payload, and the note even wrote down what
    ; the reference does — *`Node.Text()` is ONE function over both kinds*. Two
    ; slices later `is_module_exports_access_expression` handed it the answer of
    ; GetElementOrPropertyAccessName, which is an identifier for a property access
    ; and a STRING LITERAL for an element access, so `module["exports"]["d"] = {}`
    ; was not recognised as a CommonJS export at all — the same failure shape as
    ; the private identifier, for the same reason: a payload nobody listed answers
    ; null here and length 0 below, which is a well-formed FALSE and not a crash.
    ;
    ; So the function is now the reference's `Text()` comparison and is named after
    ; it, and it reads the text through the two accessors that already own the
    ; payload lists rather than carrying a third copy of one. What it does NOT
    ; cover is `Text()`'s two CONSTRUCTED answers — a MetaProperty (its name's
    ; text) and a JsxNamespacedName (namespace, colon, name) — because nothing
    ; compares either: get_declaration_name builds the JSX name itself, in a
    ; buffer, since it needs the bytes and not a verdict.
    function text_is(n: ref[AstNode]?, t: pointer[const_char]) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        var at identifier_text_of(n)
        var alen identifier_text_length_of(n)
        if at = null
        {
            set at: literal_text_of(n)
            set alen: literal_text_length_of(n)
        }
        var i: int 0
        while i < alen
        {
            let c *(t + i) as int
            if c = 0
                return false
            if (*(at + i) as int) <> c
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        (*(t + alen) as int) = 0
    }

    ; The reference's `typeExpression.Type()`. WrappedTypeData is shared by a
    ; couple of dozen one-slot kinds and a `when` arm names the union TAG, so
    ; this asks for exactly the one kind that is meant rather than for "whatever
    ; has one slot" — which is also the safer reading: a caller that reached
    ; here with an ArrayType wants array_element_type_of below and would
    ; otherwise get a silent right-looking answer.
    function jsdoc_type_expression_inner_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when w: JSDocTypeExpression
                return w.inner
        null
    }

    ; The same one slot on a JSDocVariadicType — a DIFFERENT arm over the same
    ; record, which is exactly why it needs its own reader (§3.5bb). The
    ; reparser asks it for `@param {...T} x`, where the reference writes
    ; `variadicType.Type` rather than going through TypeExpression at all.
    function jsdoc_variadic_inner_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when w: JSDocVariadicType
                return w.inner
        null
    }

    function array_element_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when a: ArrayType
                return a.inner
        null
    }

    ; ── the type-node accessors of slice 61 ─────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Each is ONE arm, and that is exactness rather than narrowing (§3.5br): the
    ; reference reaches every one of these slots through a downcast from the single
    ; record that has it — `node.AsTypeOperatorNode().Type`,
    ; `mappedTypeNode.NameType`, `node.AsInferTypeNode().TypeParameter` — and not
    ; through a shared `Node.X()`. A second arm here would be a claim the reference
    ; does not make, and §3.5bk's rule (a narrowed accessor answers a WRONG answer
    ; rather than crashing) cuts both ways: a WIDENED one does too.

    ; The OPERATOR of a TypeOperatorNode — `keyof`, `unique` or `readonly`. Answers
    ; KindUnknown for any other kind, which no caller can reach:
    ; checkGrammarTypeOperatorNode is entered from the TypeOperator arm alone.
    function type_operator_operator_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when t: TypeOperator
                return t.operator_kind
        KindUnknown
    }

    function type_operator_inner_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: TypeOperator
                return t.inner
        null
    }

    ; The two remaining one-slot type nodes getArrayElementTypeNode walks through
    ; — it recurses through a PARENTHESIZED type and through the REST element of a
    ; one-element tuple, which is how `readonly (T[])` and `[...T[]]` are both
    ; recognised as array-like SYNTAX without asking for a type.
    function parenthesized_type_inner_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when p: ParenthesizedType
                return p.inner
        null
    }

    function rest_type_inner_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when r: RestType
                return r.inner
        null
    }

    ; An InferTypeNode's TypeParameter. The slot is a TypeParameter DECLARATION and
    ; not a type, which is why checkInferType hands it to checkSourceElement and is
    ; the first caller in this port to reach the TypeParameter arm of that switch.
    function infer_type_parameter_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when i: InferType
                return i.inner
        null
    }

    ; Node.MemberList()/Node.Members(), whole — six kinds, and it REPLACES the two
    ; narrow readers slice 61 wrote (`type_literal_members_of` and
    ; `mapped_type_members_of`).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THAT IS §3.5br ARRIVING ON SCHEDULE, and the retired comment is the
    ; evidence: it read *the reference's `node.Members()` at the one kind
    ; checkTypeLiteral is entered with*, and slice 62's checkGrammarProperty asks
    ; the same question of a node whose kind it does NOT know — a property's PARENT,
    ; which is a class, an interface, a type literal or a mapped type, and the
    ; reference spells all four `node.Parent.Members()`. A third narrow reader would
    ; have been the same accessor a third time.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference PANICS on a seventh kind and this answers null, which is
    ; §3.5bk's convention: every caller reads a null list as an empty one, because
    ; `list_count` answers 0 and `child_in_list` null for it.
    ;
    ; ★ The members of a MAPPED type are the reference's own recovery slot: the
    ; grammar forbids them (`A mapped type may not declare properties or methods`),
    ; so the list is normally empty and the parser fills it only for a malformed
    ; `{ [K in T]: U; x: number }`. That is the one shape in which a PropertySignature
    ; has a mapped type for a parent, and it is exactly the shape the first report of
    ; checkGrammarProperty is about.
    function member_list_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when cd: ClassDeclaration
                return cd.members
            when ce: ClassExpression
                return ce.members
            when id: InterfaceDeclaration
                return id.members
            when ed: EnumDeclaration
                return ed.members
            when tl: TypeLiteral
                return tl.members
            when mt: MappedType
                return mt.members
        null
    }

    ; The three MappedType slots checkMappedType reads that no other accessor
    ; answers. Its `Type` is on type_of already and its `QuestionToken` on
    ; question_token_of; these three are not.
    function mapped_type_parameter_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when m: MappedType
                return m.type_parameter
        null
    }

    function mapped_type_name_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when m: MappedType
                return m.name_type
        null
    }

    function template_literal_type_spans_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: TemplateLiteralType
                return t.template_spans
        null
    }

    ; The TYPE of a TemplateLiteralTypeSpan. The port shares TemplateSpanData with
    ; the expression form, so the slot is called `expression` and holds a type here
    ; — the same name-not-a-difference `elements_of` records for a tuple's `types`.
    ; It is an accessor of its own rather than an arm of `type_of` for the reason
    ; template_span_literal is one: the shared record makes the INDEX ambiguous and
    ; the KIND is what disambiguates.
    function template_literal_type_span_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: TemplateLiteralTypeSpan
                return t.expression
        null
    }

    function import_type_attributes_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when it: ImportType
                return it.attributes
        null
    }

    function type_reference_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: TypeReference
                return t.type_name
        null
    }

    function type_reference_arguments_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: TypeReference
                return t.type_arguments
        null
    }

    ; The ExtendsType slot of a conditional type — `T extends U ? X : Y`'s U.
    ;
    ; ★ ONE ARM, and that is exact rather than narrowed (§3.5br): upstream reaches
    ; this slot through `parent.AsConditionalTypeNode().ExtendsType`, a downcast
    ; from the one record that has it, not through a shared `Node.X()` accessor
    ; defined per data record. Its reader is getInferTypeContainer, which asks the
    ; question that MATTERS here — *is this node the conditional's extendsType*.
    ;
    ; ★ Checker slice 69 adds the TRUE-type accessor beside it, and the note that
    ; used to stand here said such a thing would be a slot with no reader (§3.5be).
    ; getConditionalFlowTypeOfType is that reader, and it asks the same shape of
    ; question about the other slot: *is this node the conditional's trueType*.
    function conditional_extends_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when c: ConditionalType
                return c.extends_type
        null
    }

    function conditional_true_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when c: ConditionalType
                return c.true_type
        null
    }

    ; The remaining three slots of a conditional type and the two of an indexed
    ; access — mayResolveTypeAlias (slice 95) walks every one of them, and it is
    ; the first reader any of the five has had. The two that already existed
    ; (extendsType, trueType) arrived with the readers their own notes named.
    ; JSDocData.tags — the tag list of one JSDoc tree. Its reader is
    ; GetJSDocDeprecatedTag (slice 95); the dump reaches the same nodes through the
    ; generic child walk, which is why the slot has had no named accessor until now.
    function jsdoc_tag_list_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when jd: JSDoc
                return jd.tags
        null
    }

    function conditional_check_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when c: ConditionalType
                return c.check_type
        null
    }

    function conditional_false_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when c: ConditionalType
                return c.false_type
        null
    }

    function indexed_access_object_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ia: IndexedAccessType
                return ia.object_type
        null
    }

    function indexed_access_index_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ia: IndexedAccessType
                return ia.index_type
        null
    }

    function qualified_left_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when q: QualifiedName
                return q.left
        null
    }

    function qualified_right_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when q: QualifiedName
                return q.right
        null
    }

    ; The TagName slot, shared by every JSDoc tag shape — the reference's
    ; `child.TagName()`, which two error sites report the RANGE of.
    function jsdoc_tag_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: JSDocUnknownTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocPublicTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocPrivateTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocProtectedTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocReadonlyTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocOverrideTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocDeprecatedTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocTypeTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocReturnTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocSeeTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocImplementsTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocAugmentsTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocSatisfiesTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocThrowsTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocThisTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocOverloadTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocTemplateTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocImportTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocTypedefTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocCallbackTag
                return t.tag_name
            when t: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return t.tag_name
        null
    }

    ; The TypeExpression of a @type tag — read back by parseTypedefTag when a
    ; nested `@type` decides the typedef's own type.
    function jsdoc_type_tag_expression_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: JSDocTypeTag
                return t.node
        null
    }

    ; The NAME of a @param/@property tag — the reference's `child.Name()`, which
    ; parseChildParameterOrPropertyTag compares against the parent's.
    function jsdoc_param_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return t.name
        null
    }

    ; ── ast.TagNamesAreEquivalent ───────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Slice 20. `<a.b>…</a.b>` closes `<a.b>`; `<a.b>…</a.c>` does not. The
    ; reference asks this three times per element — once to decide whether to
    ; report a mismatch, and twice inside parseJsxChildren to decide whether an
    ; unclosed child has stolen its parent's closing tag — so it is a real part of
    ; the grammar and not a checker convenience.
    ;
    ; It lives here, with the arms, for the reason binary_operator_kind does:
    ; `choose` over the payload belongs in the file that declares the payload,
    ; and putting it in parser.scaly would put a second copy of the arm list in a
    ; file with no reason to know one.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference PANICS on any other kind, which is sound there because
    ; parseJsxElementName can only build these four. Here the fall-through is
    ; `false` — a missing identifier from error recovery is an Identifier with
    ; empty text and reaches the first arm, so the difference is unobservable and
    ; a trap in a tree walk is worse than a mismatch report.
    function tag_names_are_equivalent(lhs: ref[AstNode]?, rhs: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if lhs = null
            return false
        if rhs = null
            return false
        if lhs.kind <> rhs.kind
            return false

        if lhs.kind = KindIdentifier
            return identifier_texts_equal(lhs, rhs)

        ; `<this>` — a KeywordExpression, i.e. a Token node whose kind says which
        ; keyword. Two of them are equivalent by having the same kind, which the
        ; test above already established.
        if lhs.kind = KindThisKeyword
            return true

        if lhs.kind = KindJsxNamespacedName
        {
            let ln namespaced_namespace_of(lhs)
            let rn namespaced_namespace_of(rhs)
            if not identifier_texts_equal(ln, rn)
                return false
            return identifier_texts_equal(namespaced_name_of(lhs), namespaced_name_of(rhs))
        }

        if lhs.kind = KindPropertyAccessExpression
        {
            if not identifier_texts_equal(property_access_name_of(lhs), property_access_name_of(rhs))
                return false
            return tag_names_are_equivalent(property_access_expression_of(lhs), property_access_expression_of(rhs))
        }

        false
    }

    ; The reference's `Node.Text()` for the one kind a tag name compares by text.
    ; A non-Identifier answers a zero-length text, which compares equal to
    ; another one — reachable only through the fall-through above.
    function identifier_texts_equal(a: ref[AstNode]?, b: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if a = null
            return b = null
        if b = null
            return false
        var at null as pointer[char]
        var alen 0
        choose a.data
            when id: Identifier
            {
                set at: id.text
                set alen: id.text_len
            }
        var bt null as pointer[char]
        var blen 0
        choose b.data
            when id: Identifier
            {
                set bt: id.text
                set blen: id.text_len
            }
        if alen <> blen
            return false
        var i: int 0
        while i < alen
        {
            if (*(at + i) as int) <> (*(bt + i) as int)
                return false
            set i: i + 1
        }
        true
    }

    function namespaced_namespace_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        choose n.data
            when jnn: JsxNamespacedName
                return jnn.namespace_name
        null
    }

    function namespaced_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        choose n.data
            when jnn: JsxNamespacedName
                return jnn.name
        null
    }

    function property_access_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        choose n.data
            when pa: PropertyAccessExpression
                return pa.name
        null
    }

    function property_access_expression_of(n: ref[AstNode]) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        choose n.data
            when pa: PropertyAccessExpression
                return pa.expression
        null
    }

    ; The TAG NAME of an opening element, a self-closing element or a closing
    ; element — the reference's `Node.TagName()`, which parseJsx needs on nodes it
    ; has already finished. A fragment has none and answers null, which is what
    ; makes the fragment arms of the two mismatch tests fall away.
    function jsx_tag_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when joe: JsxOpeningElement
                return joe.tag_name
            when jsce: JsxSelfClosingElement
                return jsce.tag_name
            when jce: JsxClosingElement
                return jce.inner
        null
    }

    ; A JsxElement's three slots, read back after the node is built. The
    ; reference reaches into `lastChild.AsJsxElement()` directly; here the arms
    ; live with the union and the parser asks.
    function jsx_element_opening_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when je: JsxElement
                return je.opening_element
        null
    }

    function jsx_element_closing_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when je: JsxElement
                return je.closing_element
        null
    }

    function jsx_element_children_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when je: JsxElement
                return je.children
        null
    }

    function jsx_element_children_end_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when je: JsxElement
                return je.children_end
        0
    }


    ; ── the JSDoc reparser's accessors and mutators (slice 22) ───────────────
    ;
    ; The reference reaches a node's slots two ways, and both have to be answered
    ; here. `Node.Type()`, `Node.Name()`, `Node.Expression()` … are generic
    ; accessors over the interface — a `switch node.Kind` in ast.go — and
    ; `parent.AsMutable().SetType(t)` is the generic WRITE, which Go can express
    ; because the payload is a pointer to a struct and the mutation reaches the
    ; node in the tree.
    ;
    ; ★★★ HERE THE PAYLOAD IS A UNION BY VALUE, so a write is a READ-MODIFY-WRITE
    ; of the whole arm: bind the payload, set the field on the BINDING, and store
    ; the arm back into `n.data`. Mutating through the `choose` binding directly
    ; is not relied on — the binding's write-through is not something this port
    ; wants to depend on, and the arm store is one line either way.
    ;
    ; Both halves live here rather than in parser.scaly for the reason every
    ; other payload question does: `choose` lives with the arms, and a second
    ; copy of the arm list in a file with no reason to know it is how an arm goes
    ; missing.

    ; Node.Type() — every kind the reparser asks it of, and no others. A kind
    ; that has no type slot answers null, which is what the reference's default
    ; arm does.
    function type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when vd: VariableDeclaration
                return vd.type_node
            when pd: PropertyDeclaration
                return pd.type_node
            when ps: PropertySignature
                return ps.type_node
            when pa: PropertyAssignment
                return pa.type_node
            when spa: ShorthandPropertyAssignment
                return spa.type_node
            when ea: ExportAssignment
                return ea.type_node
            when pm: Parameter
                return pm.type_node
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.type_node
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.type_node
            when af: ArrowFunction
                return af.type_node
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.type_node
            when ctd: ConstructorDeclaration
                return ctd.type_node
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.type_node
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.type_node
            when ms: MethodSignature
                return ms.type_node
            when cs: CallSignature
                return cs.type_node
            when ks: ConstructSignature
                return ks.type_node
            when ix: IndexSignatureDeclaration
                return ix.type_node
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return be.type_node
            when ta: TypeAliasDeclaration
                return ta.type_node
            when jta: JSTypeAliasDeclaration
                return jta.type_node

            ; ★ Slice 24 completed this over every arm whose record carries the
            ; slot. `Node.Type()` upstream is `n.data.Type()`, i.e. it is defined
            ; for each of these and not for a chosen few — and checkJSSyntax asks
            ; it of an AsExpression and a SatisfiesExpression, neither of which
            ; was here. A narrowed accessor answers a wrong ANSWER rather than
            ; crashing (§3.5bk), so the arms that were merely unreached were the
            ; same code as the two that were needed.
            when tprd: TypePredicate
                return tprd.type_node
            when ntmb: NamedTupleMember
                return ntmb.type_node
            when fnty: FunctionType
                return fnty.type_node
            when ctty: ConstructorType
                return ctty.type_node
            when tasx: TypeAssertionExpression
                return tasx.type_node
            when asex: AsExpression
                return asex.type_node
            when satx: SatisfiesExpression
                return satx.type_node
            when mpty: MappedType
                return mpty.type_node
            when jsig: JSDocSignature
                return jsig.type_node
        null
    }

    ; Node.Name(), for the kinds the reparser asks.
    function name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when vd: VariableDeclaration
                return vd.name
            when pd: PropertyDeclaration
                return pd.name
            when pa: PropertyAssignment
                return pa.name
            when spa: ShorthandPropertyAssignment
                return spa.name
            when pm: Parameter
                return pm.name
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.name
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.name
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.name
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.name
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.name
            when cl: ClassDeclaration
                return cl.name
            when ce: ClassExpression
                return ce.name
            when mdl: ModuleDeclaration
                return mdl.name
            when tp: TypeParameter
                return tp.name
            when ta: TypeAliasDeclaration
                return ta.name
            when jta: JSTypeAliasDeclaration
                return jta.name
            when jtd: JSDocTypedefTag
                return jtd.name
            when jcb: JSDocCallbackTag
                return jcb.name
            when jp: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return jp.name

            ; ★ Slice 24 completed this too, and it is the same rule one accessor
            ; over: `Node.Name()` is `n.data.Name()`, defined for every record
            ; that has the slot. checkJSSyntax reports an interface and an enum
            ; at their NAME, and those two arms were the ones missing — the other
            ; fifteen are the same one-line shape and were left out only because
            ; nothing had asked yet.
            when ntmb: NamedTupleMember
                return ntmb.name
            when pacc: PropertyAccessExpression
                return pacc.name
            when mprp: MetaProperty
                return mprp.name
            when msig: MethodSignature
                return msig.name
            when psig: PropertySignature
                return psig.name
            when intf: InterfaceDeclaration
                return intf.name
            when enud: EnumDeclaration
                return enud.name
            when enum_member: EnumMember
                return enum_member.name
            when impeq: ImportEqualsDeclaration
                return impeq.name
            when impcl: ImportClause
                return impcl.name
            when impsp: ImportSpecifier
                return impsp.name
            when impat: ImportAttribute
                return impat.name
            when nsexp: NamespaceExportDeclaration
                return nsexp.name
            when expsp: ExportSpecifier
                return expsp.name

            ; ★ Slice 29, and both are §3.5bb's shape rather than an oversight of
            ; the same kind as the two above: NamespaceImport and NamespaceExport
            ; share `WrappedTypeData` with a dozen wrapper kinds, so their one slot
            ; is spelled `inner` and neither can be reached by an arm written for
            ; another tag. The reference gives each of them a `name` field
            ; (`NamespaceImport{ name *IdentifierNode }`,
            ; `NamespaceExport{ name *ModuleExportName }`) and getDeclarationName
            ; asks for it — `import * as ns from "m"` and `export * as ns from "m"`
            ; each declare a symbol under that name.
            when nsimp: NamespaceImport
                return nsimp.inner
            when nsexpc: NamespaceExport
                return nsexpc.inner
            when bindel: BindingElement
                return bindel.name
            when jsxat: JsxAttribute
                return jsxat.name
            when jsxns: JsxNamespacedName
                return jsxns.name
        null
    }

    ; Node.Expression(), for the kinds the reparser asks.
    ; ★★★ PORTED WHOLE, and the reason is that the reference PANICS on a kind it
    ; does not list while this answers null — so a missing arm is not a crash but
    ; a silently wrong ANSWER, and every caller here reads null as "there is no
    ; expression". It was written with the eight arms its callers were thought to
    ; reach, and `ElementAccessExpression` was not among them: with
    ; `is_entity_name_expression_js` and `is_module_exports_access_expression`
    ; both asking it of exactly that kind, `/** @type object */ this["a"] = foo;`
    ; lost its whole reparse (§3.5bk). The narrowing saved nothing — these are
    ; one-line arms — and cost a defect no yardstick could attribute to this
    ; function, which is §3.5bb's rule reaching an ACCESSOR for the third time.
    ;
    ; Every kind of the reference's Node.Expression() switch is here except
    ; KindPartiallyEmittedExpression, which is a TRANSFORM node no parser builds.
    function expression_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when pae: PropertyAccessExpression
                return pae.expression
            when eae: ElementAccessExpression
                return eae.expression
            when pe: ParenthesizedExpression
                return pe.inner
            when ce: CallExpression
                return ce.expression
            when ne: NewExpression
                return ne.expression
            when ewta: ExpressionWithTypeArguments
                return ewta.expression
            when cpn: ComputedPropertyName
                return cpn.inner
            when nne: NonNullExpression
                return nne.inner
            when tae: TypeAssertionExpression
                return tae.expression
            when ae: AsExpression
                return ae.expression
            when se: SatisfiesExpression
                return se.expression
            when toe: TypeOfExpression
                return toe.inner
            when sa: SpreadAssignment
                return sa.inner
            when sel: SpreadElement
                return sel.inner
            when ts: TemplateSpan
                return ts.expression
            when de: DeleteExpression
                return de.inner
            when ve: VoidExpression
                return ve.inner
            when awe: AwaitExpression
                return awe.inner
            when ye: YieldExpression
                return ye.expression
            when ifs: IfStatement
                return ifs.expression
            when ds: DoStatement
                return ds.expression
            when ws: WhileStatement
                return ws.expression
            when wits: WithStatement
                return wits.expression
            when fis: ForInStatement
                return fis.expression
            when fos: ForOfStatement
                return fos.expression
            when ss: SwitchStatement
                return ss.expression
            when cc: CaseClause
                return cc.expression
            when es: ExpressionStatement
                return es.expression
            when rs: ReturnStatement
                return rs.expression
            when tstm: ThrowStatement
                return tstm.expression
            when emr: ExternalModuleReference
                return emr.inner
            when ea: ExportAssignment
                return ea.expression
            when dec: Decorator
                return dec.inner
            when jxe: JsxExpression
                return jxe.expression
            when jssa: JsxSpreadAttribute
                return jssa.inner
        null
    }

    ; Node.Initializer(), at the reference's FULL breadth. It was written with the
    ; four arms the reparser asks for, and slice 35 widened it to the ten the
    ; reference lists — §3.5bk, for the third accessor: the reference PANICS on a
    ; kind it does not name while this answers null, so the missing arm is not a
    ; deferred defect but a silently wrong ANSWER. The one that came due was
    ; BindingElement, which bindBindingElementFlow asks for BY NAME and out of
    ; child order — and those four explicit binds REPLACE the child walk, so a null
    ; here would not have bound the initializer at all (control c36 is that null,
    ; and it is red).
    function initializer_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when vd: VariableDeclaration
                return vd.initializer
            when pm: Parameter
                return pm.initializer
            when be: BindingElement
                return be.initializer
            when pd: PropertyDeclaration
                return pd.initializer
            when ps: PropertySignature
                return ps.initializer
            when pa: PropertyAssignment
                return pa.initializer
            when em: EnumMember
                return em.initializer
            when fs: ForStatement
                return fs.initializer
            when fis: ForInStatement
                return fis.initializer
            when fos: ForOfStatement
                return fos.initializer
            when ja: JsxAttribute
                return ja.initializer
        null
    }

    ; Node.ModifierNodes(), for the kinds the reparser asks.
    function modifiers_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.modifiers
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.modifiers
            when ctd: ConstructorDeclaration
                return ctd.modifiers
            when pd: PropertyDeclaration
                return pd.modifiers
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.modifiers
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.modifiers
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return be.modifiers
            when tp: TypeParameter
                return tp.modifiers

            ; ★★★ Slice 24 completed this, and here the narrowing was not merely
            ; unreached — it was WRONG for two readers that already existed.
            ; `Node.ModifierNodes()` is per-record upstream, checkJSDecoratorSyntax
            ; asks it of EVERY node in a JavaScript file, and the second half of
            ; checkJSSyntax asks it of a class, a variable statement and a
            ; parameter. None of those three had an arm. The reparser's
            ; `reparse_jsdoc_signature` and `reparse_modifier_tag` were reading it
            ; of an arrow function, a function expression and a property signature
            ; and getting null.
            when vstm: VariableStatement
                return vstm.modifiers
            when tald: TypeAliasDeclaration
                return tald.modifiers
            when prm: Parameter
                return prm.modifiers
            when ctty: ConstructorType
                return ctty.modifiers
            when pasg: PropertyAssignment
                return pasg.modifiers
            when shpa: ShorthandPropertyAssignment
                return shpa.modifiers
            when arrf: ArrowFunction
                return arrf.modifiers
            when fnex: FunctionExpression
                return fnex.modifiers
            when clsd: ClassDeclaration
                return clsd.modifiers
            when clse: ClassExpression
                return clse.modifiers
            when cssb: ClassStaticBlockDeclaration
                return cssb.modifiers
            when ixsg: IndexSignatureDeclaration
                return ixsg.modifiers
            when msig: MethodSignature
                return msig.modifiers
            when psig: PropertySignature
                return psig.modifiers
            when intf: InterfaceDeclaration
                return intf.modifiers
            when enud: EnumDeclaration
                return enud.modifiers
            when modd: ModuleDeclaration
                return modd.modifiers
            when impd: ImportDeclaration
                return impd.modifiers
            when impeq: ImportEqualsDeclaration
                return impeq.modifiers
            when expa: ExportAssignment
                return expa.modifiers
            when nsexp: NamespaceExportDeclaration
                return nsexp.modifiers
            when expd: ExportDeclaration
                return expd.modifiers
            when jstad: JSTypeAliasDeclaration
                return jstad.modifiers
            when jsimp: JSImportDeclaration
                return jsimp.modifiers
        null
    }

    ; Node.Parameters() and Node.TypeParameters().
    ;
    ; ★★★ parameters_of covers every kind the reference's `FunctionLikeData()`
    ; does, and until slice 40 it covered the ones `getFunctionLikeHost` can
    ; answer — two fewer, FunctionType and ConstructorType. That narrowing was
    ; MEASURED reachable rather than argued away: the binder's binding-pattern arm
    ; needs `node.Parent.Parameters()` and a `([a]: T) => void` parents its
    ; parameters on a FunctionType, so two stage-1 units reported a port gap the
    ; moment the arm existed. §3.5br's lesson, paid a fourth time — an accessor
    ; narrowed to today's callers is re-narrowed by tomorrow's.
    ;
    ; ★ type_parameters_of is NOT widened with it, and the difference is that
    ; nothing asks it of those two kinds yet. It is the same narrowing waiting at
    ; the same place; when a slice needs it, the arms are `ft.type_parameters` and
    ; `ct.type_parameters`.
    function parameters_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.parameters
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.parameters
            when af: ArrowFunction
                return af.parameters
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.parameters
            when ctd: ConstructorDeclaration
                return ctd.parameters
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.parameters
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.parameters
            when js: JSDocSignature
                return js.parameters
            when ms: MethodSignature
                return ms.parameters
            when cs: CallSignature
                return cs.parameters
            when ks: ConstructSignature
                return ks.parameters
            when ix: IndexSignatureDeclaration
                return ix.parameters
            when ft: FunctionType
                return ft.parameters
            when ct: ConstructorType
                return ct.parameters
        null
    }

    ; The reference's Node.TypeParameterList(): five explicit cases —
    ; ClassDeclaration, ClassExpression, InterfaceDeclaration, the two type-alias
    ; kinds, JSDocTemplateTag — and a default arm that answers FunctionLikeData's
    ; slot, i.e. every function-like record.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT WAS NARROWED TO ITS CALLERS AND SLICE 57 WIDENED IT — §3.5br's rule a
    ; fifth time. The arms this port had were the ones its two readers were
    ; believed to reach (a class-like's grammar list, a function declaration's), so
    ; an INTERFACE and a TYPE ALIAS answered null, and the interface arm of that
    ; slice would have walked past checkTypeParameters on every generic interface
    ; in the corpus without a word. The seven added arms are the reference's own
    ; switch, not the new caller's needs.
    ;
    ; ★ IndexSignatureDeclaration is EXACT rather than narrowed, the way
    ; postfix_token_of's three missing arms are: the reference reaches it through
    ; FunctionLikeBase, but `[k: string]: T` has no place to write `<T>` and the
    ; reference's own parser passes no type parameters to NewIndexSignatureDeclaration,
    ; so this port's record has no such slot. It expires the moment anything writes one.
    function type_parameters_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.type_parameters
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.type_parameters
            when af: ArrowFunction
                return af.type_parameters
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.type_parameters
            when ctd: ConstructorDeclaration
                return ctd.type_parameters
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.type_parameters
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.type_parameters
            when cl: ClassDeclaration
                return cl.type_parameters
            when ce: ClassExpression
                return ce.type_parameters
            when jt: JSDocTemplateTag
                return jt.type_parameters
            when ms: MethodSignature
                return ms.type_parameters
            when cs: CallSignature
                return cs.type_parameters
            when ks: ConstructSignature
                return ks.type_parameters
            when intf: InterfaceDeclaration
                return intf.type_parameters
            when ta: TypeAliasDeclaration
                return ta.type_parameters
            when jta: JSTypeAliasDeclaration
                return jta.type_parameters
            when ft: FunctionType
                return ft.type_parameters
            when ct: ConstructorType
                return ct.type_parameters
            when jss: JSDocSignature
                return jss.type_parameters
        null
    }

    ; ArrowFunction.EqualsGreaterThanToken (slice 100) — the only node whose own
    ; leading trivia carries a grammar rule, which is why it needs a reader at all.
    function equals_greater_than_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when af: ArrowFunction
                return af.equals_greater_than_token
        null
    }

    function full_signature_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.full_signature
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.full_signature
            when af: ArrowFunction
                return af.full_signature
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.full_signature
            when ctd: ConstructorDeclaration
                return ctd.full_signature
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.full_signature
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.full_signature
            when ms: MethodSignature
                return ms.full_signature
            when cs: CallSignature
                return cs.full_signature
            when ks: ConstructSignature
                return ks.full_signature
            when ix: IndexSignatureDeclaration
                return ix.full_signature
        null
    }

    function body_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fd: FunctionDeclaration
                return fd.body
            when fe: FunctionExpression
                return fe.body
            when af: ArrowFunction
                return af.body
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.body
            when ctd: ConstructorDeclaration
                return ctd.body
            when ga: GetAccessorDeclaration
                return ga.body
            when sa: SetAccessorDeclaration
                return sa.body
            when mdl: ModuleDeclaration
                return mdl.body
        null
    }

    ; ModuleDeclaration.Keyword — `module`, `namespace` or `global`, which is what
    ; IsGlobalScopeAugmentation reads and therefore what IsAmbientModule reads
    ; through it (binder.scaly's is_ambient_module, slice 28). One slot, one
    ; reader: the field has been on the record since the parser slice that built
    ; module declarations, and this is the first question asked of it (§3.5be read
    ; the other way round — a field with no reader is only declared, and now it
    ; has one).
    function module_keyword_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when mdl: ModuleDeclaration
                return mdl.keyword
        KindUnknown
    }

    ; ExportDeclaration.ExportClause — the `{ a, b }` or the `* as ns` of an
    ; export, and null for a bare `export * from "m"`. bindExportDeclaration reads
    ; it twice: its ABSENCE selects the `__export` star symbol, and a
    ; NamespaceExport in it is declared as an alias in the container's exports.
    function export_clause_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when exd: ExportDeclaration
                return exd.export_clause
        null
    }

    ; A JSDoc tag's TypeExpression slot. The three tag SHAPES that carry one, and
    ; the shape decides which slot it is — the one-slot tags call it
    ; TypeExpression outright, the named tags (@typedef, @callback) put it in the
    ; middle slot, and the parameter/property tag has its own field.
    function jsdoc_type_expression_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: JSDocTypeTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocReturnTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocThisTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocSatisfiesTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocThrowsTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocOverloadTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocSeeTag
                return t.node
            when nt: JSDocTypedefTag
                return nt.type_expression
            when nt: JSDocCallbackTag
                return nt.type_expression
            when pt: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return pt.type_expression
        null
    }

    ; A JSDocImplementsTag's / JSDocAugmentsTag's ClassName — the same one slot
    ; the shared record carries.
    function jsdoc_class_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: JSDocImplementsTag
                return t.node
            when t: JSDocAugmentsTag
                return t.node
        null
    }

    ; Node.CommentList() for the JSDoc node ITSELF, plus a forward to the tag
    ; arms below (slice 78). The reference writes both halves as ONE switch whose
    ; first case is KindJSDoc; here the tag half already existed under the name
    ; jsdoc_tag_comment_of, and controls-slice22.sh patches that function BY NAME,
    ; so the new arm goes in front rather than into it.
    ;
    ; ★ checkJSDocComments is called with a JSDoc and again with each of its TAGS,
    ; so the caller needs the union and not either half.
    function jsdoc_comment_list_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when d: JSDoc
                return d.comment
        AstNode.jsdoc_tag_comment_of(n)
    }

    ; A JSDoc tag's comment list — the reference's Node.CommentList().
    function jsdoc_tag_comment_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when t: JSDocUnknownTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocPublicTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocPrivateTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocProtectedTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocReadonlyTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocOverrideTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocDeprecatedTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocTypeTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocReturnTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocSeeTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocImplementsTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocAugmentsTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocSatisfiesTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocThrowsTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocThisTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocOverloadTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocTemplateTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocImportTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocTypedefTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocCallbackTag
                return t.comment
            when t: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return t.comment
        null
    }

    ; A JSDoc comment's Tags list.
    function jsdoc_tags_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when d: JSDoc
                return d.tags
        null
    }

    ; JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag's two flag fields.
    function jsdoc_param_is_bracketed(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when pt: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return pt.is_bracketed
        false
    }

    function jsdoc_param_is_name_first(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when pt: JSDocParameterOrPropertyTag
                return pt.is_name_first
        false
    }

    ; JSDocTypeLiteral's two slots.
    function jsdoc_type_literal_tags_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when tl: JSDocTypeLiteral
                return tl.property_tags
        null
    }

    function jsdoc_type_literal_is_array(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when tl: JSDocTypeLiteral
                return tl.is_array_type
        false
    }

    ; JSDocTemplateTag's constraint slot.
    function jsdoc_template_constraint_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when jt: JSDocTemplateTag
                return jt.constraint
        null
    }

    ; JSDocImportTag's three remaining slots.
    function jsdoc_import_clause_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when it: JSDocImportTag
                return it.import_clause
        null
    }

    function jsdoc_import_specifier_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when it: JSDocImportTag
                return it.module_specifier
        null
    }

    function jsdoc_import_attributes_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when it: JSDocImportTag
                return it.attributes
        null
    }

    ; A VariableStatement's declaration list, and a declaration list's
    ; declarations — two hops the reparser makes on every @type and @satisfies.
    function variable_statement_list_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when vs: VariableStatement
                return vs.declaration_list
        null
    }

    function variable_declarations_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when dl: VariableDeclarationList
                return dl.declarations
        null
    }

    ; The reference's getHeritageClauses — three cases, ClassDeclaration,
    ; ClassExpression and InterfaceDeclaration, nil for anything else.
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT HAD TWO OF THE THREE AND ITS COMMENT NAMED A FUNCTION THAT DOES NOT
    ; EXIST. This accessor read *the reference's getClassLikeData*, and there is no
    ; such function in internal/ast; what it is a port of is getHeritageClauses,
    ; whose switch has the interface arm. So the narrowing was invisible in the one
    ; place a reader would look for it — the same §3.5br class as type_parameters_of
    ; above, with the extra twist that the comment made the arm list look
    ; DELIBERATE. ★A wrong reference NAME in an accessor's comment is worse than no
    ; comment: it answers the question a reader would otherwise go and ask.
    ;
    ; ★ Widening is safe for the three readers that existed. Both parser readers
    ; (reparse_implements_tag, reparse_augments_tag) return at
    ; `is_class_like_node(parent) = false` one line above the call, and the
    ; checker's is check_grammar_class_declaration_heritage_clauses, reached only
    ; from the class arm. The name changed with the arm because the old one would
    ; have become a lie.
    function heritage_clauses_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when cl: ClassDeclaration
                return cl.heritage_clauses
            when ce: ClassExpression
                return ce.heritage_clauses
            when intf: InterfaceDeclaration
                return intf.heritage_clauses
        null
    }

    function heritage_clause_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when hc: HeritageClause
                return hc.token
        KindUnknown
    }

    function heritage_clause_types_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when hc: HeritageClause
                return hc.types
        null
    }

    function import_clause_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when imd: ImportDeclaration
                return imd.import_clause
            when jimd: JSImportDeclaration
                return jimd.import_clause
        null
    }

    ; The Identifier payload, so a reparse can compare two names by TEXT the way
    ; findMatchingParameter does.
    function identifier_text_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns pointer[char]
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when id: Identifier
                return id.text
            when pid: PrivateIdentifier
                return pid.text
        null
    }

    function identifier_text_length_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when id: Identifier
                return id.text_len
            when pid: PrivateIdentifier
                return pid.text_len
        0
    }


    ; The LiteralData payload, slice 31. The reference's `Node.Text()` is ONE
    ; function over fourteen kinds; here the two halves are separate because the
    ; payloads are — an Identifier's text lives in IdentifierData and a literal's
    ; in LiteralData, and a `when` arm names the TAG (§3.5bb). The caller asks the
    ; one its kind test already established.
    ;
    ; ★ The arms this pair does NOT answer are the three the reference builds by
    ; STRING CONCATENATION rather than by reading a slot — MetaProperty (its
    ; name's text), JsxNamespacedName (`ns` + `:` + name) and the JSDoc text
    ; nodes (a join over a list). PrivateIdentifier is NOT among them any more —
    ; slice 35 gave it its arm above, because checkPrivateIdentifier needs the TEXT
    ; while getDeclarationName needs a SYMBOL ID; only the latter is unanswerable,
    ; and it reports at its own line before ever asking for a text.
    ; Each is reported by its caller, so a null here is never read as a name.
    ; PrefixUnaryExpression's two slots, slice 31. They live here for the reason
    ; is_signed_numeric_literal does: the payload is a union arm, and a `choose`
    ; lives with the arms. Their one caller is getDeclarationName's SIGNED numeric
    ; computed name, which is a symbol name built out of two nodes.
    function prefix_unary_operator_kind(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when pu: PrefixUnaryExpression
                return pu.operator_kind
        KindUnknown
    }

    function prefix_unary_operand_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when pu: PrefixUnaryExpression
                return pu.operand
        null
    }

    ; ── slice 35: the three accessors the last bind arms need ───────────────
    ;
    ; PostfixUnaryExpression's operand. The reference calls the field `Operand`
    ; and the record here is WrappedTypeData, shared with a dozen other one-child
    ; nodes, so the slot is `inner` — and a `when` arm names the TAG (§3.5bb),
    ; which is why this cannot ride on expression_of's DeleteExpression arm even
    ; though the two records are the very same one.
    function postfix_unary_operand_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when pxu: PostfixUnaryExpression
                return pxu.inner
        null
    }

    ; Node.Label() and Node.Statement(), both at the reference's FULL breadth
    ; rather than at the one arm checkStrictModeLabeledStatement asks for. §3.5bk
    ; is the reason and it is not a preference: the reference PANICS on a kind it
    ; does not list while these answer null, so a missing arm here does not defer
    ; a defect, it makes one — every caller reads null as "this node has no
    ; label / no statement", which is a well-formed answer.
    ;
    ; ★ `label_name`, not `label`: the word is reserved and bites a structure
    ; PROPERTY (the note on BreakOrContinueStatementData has the account).
    function label_name_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ls: LabeledStatement
                return ls.label_name
            when bs: BreakStatement
                return bs.label_name
            when cs: ContinueStatement
                return cs.label_name
        null
    }

    function statement_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ds: DoStatement
                return ds.statement
            when ws: WhileStatement
                return ws.statement
            when fs: ForStatement
                return fs.statement
            when fis: ForInStatement
                return fis.statement
            when fos: ForOfStatement
                return fos.statement
            when wits: WithStatement
                return wits.statement
            when ls: LabeledStatement
                return ls.statement
        null
    }

    ; ── slice 36: the three slots of the `for` heads ─────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference has NO generic accessor for any of these — bindForStatement
    ; and bindForInOrOfStatement reach the slots through AsForStatement() and
    ; AsForInOrOfStatement() — so §3.5bk's rule has nothing to widen to and the
    ; breadth is the RECORD's: one arm each. `await_modifier`'s record is the one
    ; both `for-in` and `for-of` are built from (ForInOrOfStatementData's header),
    ; so it answers for both kinds, and the test for WHICH of them actually has
    ; an await modifier stays in the binder arm, where the reference put it.
    ;
    ; ★ Consequence, stated rather than hidden: for_await_modifier_of's
    ; ForInStatement arm is not REACHED today — the binder asks only under the `of`
    ; kind — and it is written because the RECORD has the slot, which is §3.5bk's
    ; reason and not a caller's. Narrowing it to ForOf would make the function
    ; answer null for a node that HAS the slot, i.e. a well-formed wrong answer the
    ; day something else asks, and the checker's `for await` rules do.
    function for_condition_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fs: ForStatement
                return fs.condition
        null
    }

    function for_incrementor_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fs: ForStatement
                return fs.incrementor
        null
    }

    function for_await_modifier_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when fis: ForInStatement
                return fis.await_modifier
            when fos: ForOfStatement
                return fos.await_modifier
        null
    }

    ; A CatchClause's variable declaration, which is absent for a bare `catch { }`
    ; — the one place checkStrictModeCatchClause has a null to test.
    function catch_variable_declaration_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when cc: CatchClause
                return cc.variable_declaration
        null
    }

    ; ── slice 63: the six slots of the `if`, the `try` and the `catch` ───────
    ;
    ; None of these has a generic reader in the reference either: checkIfStatement
    ; reaches its two branches through AsIfStatement(), checkTryStatement its three
    ; through AsTryStatement() and checkCatchClause its block through
    ; AsCatchClause(). So §3.5bk's *widen to the reference's own breadth* has
    ; nothing to widen TO, and the breadth is the RECORD's — one arm each, exactly
    ; as the three `for`-head slots above are one arm each.
    ;
    ; ★ `then_statement_of` and `else_statement_of` and NOT one `branch_of(n, i)`:
    ; the two slots differ in what null MEANS (an `if` always has a then-branch and
    ; usually has no else), and a shared reader would hide that difference behind an
    ; index — the same argument ReturnStatementData carries against sharing
    ; ExpressionStatementData.
    function then_statement_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ifs: IfStatement
                return ifs.then_statement
        null
    }

    function else_statement_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ifs: IfStatement
                return ifs.else_statement
        null
    }

    function try_block_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when trs: TryStatement
                return trs.try_block
        null
    }

    function catch_clause_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when trs: TryStatement
                return trs.catch_clause
        null
    }

    function finally_block_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when trs: TryStatement
                return trs.finally_block
        null
    }

    ; A CatchClause's block, which — unlike its variable declaration — is never
    ; absent: `catch` without a body does not parse.
    function catch_block_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when cc: CatchClause
                return cc.block
        null
    }

    function literal_text_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns pointer[char]
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when l: StringLiteral
                return l.text
            when l: NumericLiteral
                return l.text
            when l: BigIntLiteral
                return l.text
            when l: NoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral
                return l.text
            when l: RegularExpressionLiteral
                return l.text
            when l: TemplateHead
                return l.text
            when l: TemplateMiddle
                return l.text
            when l: TemplateTail
                return l.text
        null
    }

    ; The literal's TOKEN FLAGS, and it is NARROWED TO ITS CALLER (§3.5br): the
    ; one reader is checkGrammarNumericLiteral's Scientific test, so only the
    ; NumericLiteral arm is written. The other seven literal arms carry the field
    ; — LiteralData is one record for all of them (see its own note) — and adding
    ; them here would be seven lines nobody reads; the arm that reads one is the
    ; arm that adds it.
    ;
    ; ★ The parser MASKS the value per kind, so a NumericLiteral's flags are
    ; `tokenFlags & TokenFlagsNumericLiteralFlags` — Scientific is in that mask,
    ; which is what makes the narrowing safe rather than merely small.
    function numeric_token_flags_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when l: NumericLiteral
                return l.token_flags
        0
    }

    function literal_text_length_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return 0
        choose n.data
            when l: StringLiteral
                return l.text_len
            when l: NumericLiteral
                return l.text_len
            when l: BigIntLiteral
                return l.text_len
            when l: NoSubstitutionTemplateLiteral
                return l.text_len
            when l: RegularExpressionLiteral
                return l.text_len
            when l: TemplateHead
                return l.text_len
            when l: TemplateMiddle
                return l.text_len
            when l: TemplateTail
                return l.text_len
        0
    }

    ; ExportDeclaration.ModuleSpecifier and the ImportDeclaration pair that shares
    ; the field, slice 31: `export { a }` with no specifier is the shape
    ; getModuleInstanceState has to tell from `export { a } from "m"`, because only
    ; the first one can be an alias of a local declaration.
    ;
    ; ★ JSDocImportTag carries the same field and keeps its own accessor
    ; (jsdoc_import_specifier_of): its caller is the reparser, which asks about the
    ; TAG and not about a declaration, and merging the two would make one of the
    ; two call sites read as though the other's question were being asked.
    function module_specifier_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when imd: ImportDeclaration
                return imd.module_specifier
            when jimd: JSImportDeclaration
                return jimd.module_specifier
            when exd: ExportDeclaration
                return exd.module_specifier
        null
    }

    ; ast.GetImportAttributes, slice 50 — the `with { type: "json" }` clause of an
    ; import or export declaration. The reference PANICS on any other kind and this
    ; answers null (§3.5bk); its two callers have both established the kind, and
    ; checkImportEqualsDeclaration — the one arm of the family whose node has no
    ; such slot — never asks, because checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration guards
    ; the question with `!IsImportEqualsDeclaration`.
    function import_attributes_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when imd: ImportDeclaration
                return imd.attributes
            when jimd: JSImportDeclaration
                return jimd.attributes
            when exd: ExportDeclaration
                return exd.attributes
        null
    }

    ; ImportClauseData's other two slots, slice 58. `Node.Name()` already answers
    ; the third, so only these two needed an accessor; both are read by
    ; checkGrammarImportClause and by nothing else yet.
    ;
    ; ★ `phase_modifier` is a KIND (TypeKeyword / DeferKeyword / Unknown), not a
    ; flag — see ImportClauseData's header for why `import type`, `import defer`
    ; and a plain import are ONE node kind here as they are upstream.
    function import_clause_phase_modifier_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns int
    {
        if n = null
            return KindUnknown
        choose n.data
            when ic: ImportClause
                return ic.phase_modifier
        KindUnknown
    }

    function named_bindings_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ic: ImportClause
                return ic.named_bindings
        null
    }

    ; ImportEqualsDeclarationData.ModuleReference, slice 58. The port already had
    ; `import_equals_reference_kind`, which is this accessor's KIND folded into the
    ; one question slice 50 asked; get_external_module_name needs the node itself.
    function module_reference_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ied: ImportEqualsDeclaration
                return ied.module_reference
        null
    }

    ; ImportAttributesData.Attributes and ImportAttributeData.Value, slice 58 —
    ; the two slots checkExternalImportOrExportDeclaration's last block walks. The
    ; NAME slot is already answered by `name_of`.
    function import_attribute_list_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ias: ImportAttributes
                return ias.attributes
        null
    }

    function import_attribute_value_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ia: ImportAttribute
                return ia.value
        null
    }

    ; ast.GetExternalModuleName, slice 58 — *the string literal that names the
    ; module*, which is a different slot in each of six kinds.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE WHOLE SWITCH IS PORTED AND NOT THE FOUR ARMS THE ONE CALLER REACHES,
    ; which is §3.5br's rule applied in advance rather than paid for a seventh
    ; time. Slice 57 found `type_parameters_of` and `heritage_clauses_of` each
    ; narrowed to the arms their readers were BELIEVED to reach, and one of the two
    ; had a comment naming a reference function that does not exist — a narrowing
    ; that reads as deliberate is worse than a missing arm, because the next reader
    ; stops asking. Today's caller establishes the kind and can only be one of the
    ; first four; the last two cost eleven lines.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference PANICS on any other kind and this answers null (§3.5bk) —
    ; and here the null is not merely tolerated: the CALLER tests for it (`moduleName
    ; == nil` is its first line), so an unhandled kind degrades to *should be a
    ; parse error, return false* rather than to a wrong report.
    function get_external_module_name(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return AstNode.module_specifier_of(n)
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return AstNode.module_specifier_of(n)
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return AstNode.module_specifier_of(n)
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
        {
            let r AstNode.module_reference_of(n)
            if r = null
                return null
            if AstNode.kind_of(r) = KindExternalModuleReference
                return AstNode.expression_of(r)
            return null
        }
        if k = KindImportType
        {
            ; getImportTypeNodeLiteral: the argument must be a LiteralType whose
            ; literal is a string — `import("m")` in TYPE position, where the
            ; parser accepts any type node in the argument slot.
            let arg AstNode.import_type_argument_of(n)
            if arg = null
                return null
            if AstNode.kind_of(arg) <> KindLiteralType
                return null
            let lit AstNode.literal_type_inner_of(arg)
            if lit = null
                return null
            if AstNode.kind_of(lit) <> KindStringLiteral
                return null
            return lit
        }
        if k = KindCallExpression
        {
            ; core.FirstOrNil(node.Arguments()) — `require("m")`.
            let args AstNode.call_arguments_of(n)
            if AstNode.list_count(args) = 0
                return null
            return AstNode.child_in_list(args, 0)
        }
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
        {
            let name AstNode.name_of(n)
            if name = null
                return null
            if AstNode.kind_of(name) <> KindStringLiteral
                return null
            return name
        }
        null
    }

    ; ImportTypeData.Argument and the LiteralType's one slot — the two hops
    ; getImportTypeNodeLiteral makes. Each is its own reader for the reason
    ; jsdoc_type_expression_inner_of's header gives: WrappedTypeData is shared by a
    ; couple of dozen kinds, so asking for the ARM is what keeps a caller that
    ; arrived with the wrong one from getting a right-looking answer.
    function import_type_argument_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when it: ImportType
                return it.argument
        null
    }

    function literal_type_inner_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when lt: LiteralType
                return lt.inner
        null
    }

    function call_arguments_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ce: CallExpression
                return ce.arguments
        null
    }

    ; Node.ElementList/Elements, slice 31, with the arms the reference has —
    ; NamedImports, NamedExports, both binding patterns, an array literal and a
    ; tuple type — whose list this port calls `types` (it shares TypeListData),
    ; which is a name and not a difference. ★ The reference PANICS on any other kind and this answers null
    ; (§3.5bk): every caller has established the kind first, so a null is
    ; unreachable rather than tolerated, and reaching it would show up as an empty
    ; list instead of as a crash.
    function elements_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when el: NamedImports
                return el.elements
            when el: NamedExports
                return el.elements
            when el: ObjectBindingPattern
                return el.elements
            when el: ArrayBindingPattern
                return el.elements
            when el: ArrayLiteralExpression
                return el.elements
            when el: TupleType
                return el.types
        null
    }

    ; Node.PropertyList/Properties, whole: the reference has exactly two arms and
    ; panics on any other kind. Both payloads are ElementListData here, so the two
    ; arms read the same slot — which is a statement about this port's records and
    ; not about the reference, where they are two distinct structs.
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 38's caller is IsExpandoInitializer, which asks it only after
    ; establishing the kind is an object literal. The JsxAttributes arm comes with
    ; it rather than after it (§3.5br): a one-arm accessor named after its only
    ; caller is what gets re-invented by the next slice.
    function properties_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ol: ObjectLiteralExpression
                return ol.elements
            when ja: JsxAttributes
                return ja.elements
        null
    }

    ; A few more one-slot reads the reparser needs.
    function binary_left_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return be.left
        null
    }

    function binary_right_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return be.right
        null
    }

    function element_access_argument_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ea: ElementAccessExpression
                return ea.argument_expression
        null
    }

    ; The conditional's two branches, slice 88. Both are read by the same three
    ; walks — isSideEffectFree, getSyntacticTruthySemantics and
    ; getSyntacticNullishnessSemantics — each of which recurses into BOTH and
    ; combines the answers, so the pair arrives together or not at all.
    ; ConditionalExpression.Condition and .ColonToken — the two slots of the
    ; ternary that had no accessor until slice 92, because nothing had ever bound
    ; a conditional expression's children BY NAME. bindConditionalExpressionFlow
    ; does: it binds the condition through bindCondition (a different entry from
    ; bind), then the `?`, then the true branch, then the `:`, then the false one.
    ; ★The question token already has one — `question_token_of` names the
    ; ConditionalExpression arm among four — which is why only two are new.
    function conditional_condition_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ce: ConditionalExpression
                return ce.condition
        null
    }

    function colon_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ce: ConditionalExpression
                return ce.colon_token
        null
    }

    function conditional_when_true_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ce: ConditionalExpression
                return ce.when_true
        null
    }

    function conditional_when_false_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when ce: ConditionalExpression
                return ce.when_false
        null
    }

    ; ── slice 33: the four slots the two flow arms read ─────────────────────
    ;
    ; The binder's BinaryExpression and CallExpression arms bind their children
    ; one slot at a time rather than through child_at, because both depart from
    ; ForEachChild's order — so each slot needs a name.

    ; The operator TOKEN, not its kind: binary_operator_kind above answers the
    ; kind, and the flow arms have to BIND the token as a child.
    function binary_operator_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return be.operator_token
        null
    }

    function binary_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when be: BinaryExpression
                return be.type_node
        null
    }

    ; The `?.` of an optional chain, over the three kinds that can carry one.
    function question_dot_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when pa: PropertyAccessExpression
                return pa.question_dot_token
            when ea: ElementAccessExpression
                return ea.question_dot_token
            when call: CallExpression
                return call.question_dot_token
        null
    }

    ; Node.Arguments() for a call. NewExpression has an argument list too and is
    ; deliberately not here: nothing this slice ports asks it, and §3.5br's
    ; lesson is about an accessor narrowed BELOW its callers, not about one that
    ; declines to answer a question nobody asks yet.
    function call_arguments_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when call: CallExpression
                return call.arguments
        null
    }

    function parameter_question_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when pm: Parameter
                return pm.question_token
        null
    }

    function shorthand_object_assignment_initializer_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when spa: ShorthandPropertyAssignment
                return spa.object_assignment_initializer
        null
    }

    ; JSDocSignature's Type slot — which holds the @returns TAG, not a type.
    function jsdoc_signature_type_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when js: JSDocSignature
                return js.type_node
        null
    }

    function type_parameter_default_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when tp: TypeParameter
                return tp.default_type
        null
    }

    ; TypeParameterDeclaration.Expression — the slot parseTypeParameter fills
    ; INSTEAD of the constraint when what follows `extends` looked like an
    ; expression rather than a type (`class C<T extends 1> {}`). It is a recovery
    ; slot: exactly one of `constraint` and `expression` is ever set.
    ;
    ; ★ It is NOT reachable through expression_of, and deliberately so — that
    ; accessor answers the reference's `Node.Expression()`, whose per-record arms do
    ; not include a type parameter (upstream reads this slot as
    ; `node.AsTypeParameterDeclaration().Expression`). Slice 60 is its first reader:
    ; checkTypeParameter reports TS1110 on its first token.
    function type_parameter_expression_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when tp: TypeParameter
                return tp.expression
        null
    }


    ; ── slice 24: what checkJSSyntax asks of a node ──────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference reports TypeScript-only syntax found in a JavaScript file
    ; from ONE function, `checkJSSyntax`, which asks a node about eleven things.
    ; Nine of them already had an accessor here; these are the two that did not,
    ; plus four kind predicates it uses. They live with the reads rather than in
    ; the parser because they are `internal/ast` functions there.

    ; Node.PostfixToken(). The reference lists NINE kinds; six of them have the
    ; slot in this port's records and three do not — EnumMember, GetAccessor and
    ; SetAccessor. ★ That is exact rather than a narrowing, and the proof is on the
    ; other side: `NewEnumMember`, `NewGetAccessorDeclaration` and
    ; `NewSetAccessorDeclaration` take no postfix argument and their factories
    ; leave the field zero, so the reference's own PARSER never sets one and those
    ; three arms answer nil for every parse there too. It expires the moment
    ; anything WRITES one — the reparser is the only candidate — and then the slot
    ; has to be added to the record before the arm can be.
    function postfix_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when md: MethodDeclaration
                return md.postfix_token
            when spa: ShorthandPropertyAssignment
                return spa.postfix_token
            when ms: MethodSignature
                return ms.postfix_token
            when ps: PropertySignature
                return ps.postfix_token
            when pa: PropertyAssignment
                return pa.postfix_token
            when prd: PropertyDeclaration
                return prd.postfix_token
        null
    }

    ; The `!` of a VARIABLE declaration — a definite-assignment assertion — and
    ; the accessor has exactly ONE arm because the records make it so, which is
    ; the §3.5br question asked and answered rather than skipped. A property's `!`
    ; does NOT belong here: the parser puts a property's `?` and its `!` in the
    ; same `postfix_token` slot, so the question there is *which* token the slot
    ; holds and is asked through postfix_token_of / question_token_of below. A
    ; VariableDeclaration has no postfix slot and no `?`, so its `!` gets a field
    ; and this reads it.
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 54's caller is checkGrammarVariableDeclaration's definite-assignment
    ; term, whose report is on the TOKEN and not on the declaration — the one place
    ; in that function where the span is not the declaration's name.
    function exclamation_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when vd: VariableDeclaration
                return vd.exclamation_token
        null
    }

    ; Node.QuestionToken(), whole: four kinds carry the token in a field of their
    ; own, and for everything else it is the POSTFIX token when that token is a
    ; question mark. The postfix slot also holds a `!` (a definite-assignment
    ; assertion), which is why the reference tests the kind and does not just
    ; return the slot — checkJSSyntax reports `?` on a property and says nothing
    ; about `!`, whose own diagnostic comes from elsewhere.
    ;
    ; ★ `parameter_question_token_of` stays: it reads the ParameterDeclaration
    ; SLOT, which is what the reparser writes to, and the reference likewise
    ; spells that one `param.QuestionToken` rather than going through here.
    function question_token_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns ref[AstNode]?
    {
        if n = null
            return null
        choose n.data
            when pm: Parameter
                return pm.question_token
            when ce: ConditionalExpression
                return ce.question_token
            when mt: MappedType
                return mt.question_token
            when ntm: NamedTupleMember
                return ntm.question_token
        let postfix postfix_token_of(n)
        if postfix = null
            return null
        if AstNode.kind_of(postfix) = KindQuestionToken
            return postfix
        null
    }

    ; Node.IsTypeOnly(). Five kinds, and the import CLAUSE is the odd one: its
    ; answer is derived from the phase modifier rather than stored, because
    ; `import type A from "m"`, `import defer A from "m"` and a plain import are
    ; one node kind under three words (ImportClauseData's header says so).
    function is_type_only_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when ied: ImportEqualsDeclaration
                return ied.is_type_only
            when isp: ImportSpecifier
                return isp.is_type_only
            when ic: ImportClause
                return ic.phase_modifier = KindTypeKeyword
            when ed: ExportDeclaration
                return ed.is_type_only
            when esp: ExportSpecifier
                return esp.is_type_only
        false
    }

    ; ExportAssignmentData.IsExportEquals — `export = x` against
    ; `export default x`, which are one kind and differ only in this field.
    function is_export_equals_of(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        choose n.data
            when ea: ExportAssignment
                return ea.is_export_equals
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsModifier / ast.IsDecorator / ast.IsQuestionToken, on a node.
    ; is_modifier_kind itself lives in the parser, where slice 3 put it and where
    ; every other caller is; this asks it of a node's kind, which is the shape the
    ; reference's IsModifier has.
    function is_decorator(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindDecorator
    }

    function is_question_token(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        AstNode.kind_of(n) = KindQuestionToken
    }

    ; ast.isFunctionLikeDeclarationKind — function-like but NOT a signature.
    ;
    ; ★★ SPLIT OUT IN SLICE 49, and the note it replaces said why it would have
    ; to be: *the reference splits it in two (isFunctionLikeDeclarationKind is also
    ; asked on its own); the second half is inlined here because this port has no
    ; other caller for it*. checkGrammarModifiers' `const` arm is that other
    ; caller — it asks whether a type parameter's PARENT is a function-like
    ; DECLARATION, where a call signature or a method signature must answer no —
    ; so the inlined form would have been a wrong answer for seven kinds. §3.5br's
    ; lesson once more: an accessor narrowed to today's callers is re-narrowed by
    ; tomorrow's.
    function is_function_like_declaration_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionExpression
            return true
        if k = KindArrowFunction
            return true
        false
    }

    function is_function_like_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        is_function_like_declaration_kind(kind_of(n))
    }

    ; ast.IsFunctionLikeKind — the signature kinds, then the declaration kinds,
    ; in the reference's own order.
    function is_function_like_kind(k: int) returns bool
    {
        if k = KindMethodSignature
            return true
        if k = KindCallSignature
            return true
        if k = KindJSDocSignature
            return true
        if k = KindConstructSignature
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionType
            return true
        if k = KindConstructorType
            return true
        is_function_like_declaration_kind(k)
    }

    function is_function_like(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        is_function_like_kind(AstNode.kind_of(n))
    }

    ; ast.IsCallLikeExpression (slice 96). ★Its BinaryExpression arm is not a kind
    ; test and that is why it is here rather than in a kind table: `a instanceof B`
    ; is call-like because the relation resolves a signature for it, and the
    ; operator token is what says so.
    function is_call_like_expression(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindJsxOpeningElement
            return true
        if k = KindJsxSelfClosingElement
            return true
        if k = KindJsxOpeningFragment
            return true
        if k = KindCallExpression
            return true
        if k = KindNewExpression
            return true
        if k = KindTaggedTemplateExpression
            return true
        if k = KindDecorator
            return true
        if k = KindBinaryExpression
            return binary_operator_kind(n) = KindInstanceOfKeyword
        false
    }

    ; ast.CanHaveDecorators and ast.CanHaveIllegalDecorators. The two sets are
    ; disjoint and both are read by checkJSDecoratorSyntax, which asks the first
    ; question first: a node that may not carry a decorator at all reports one
    ; wherever it sits, while a node that may reports one only in the wrong
    ; PLACE.
    function can_have_decorators(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindParameter
            return true
        if k = KindPropertyDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindClassExpression
            return true
        if k = KindClassDeclaration
            return true
        false
    }

    function can_have_illegal_decorators(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k AstNode.kind_of(n)
        if k = KindPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindShorthandPropertyAssignment
            return true
        if k = KindFunctionDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindConstructor
            return true
        if k = KindIndexSignature
            return true
        if k = KindClassStaticBlockDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindMissingDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindVariableStatement
            return true
        if k = KindInterfaceDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindTypeAliasDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindEnumDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindModuleDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportEqualsDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindJSImportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindNamespaceExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindExportAssignment
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.ModifierToFlag, whole. See ModifierFlags.scaly for why the flag and not
    ; a five-kind predicate.
    function modifier_to_flag(k: int) returns int
    {
        if k = KindStaticKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsStatic
        if k = KindPublicKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsPublic
        if k = KindProtectedKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsProtected
        if k = KindPrivateKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsPrivate
        if k = KindAbstractKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsAbstract
        if k = KindAccessorKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsAccessor
        if k = KindExportKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsExport
        if k = KindDeclareKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsAmbient
        if k = KindConstKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsConst
        if k = KindDefaultKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsDefault
        if k = KindAsyncKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsAsync
        if k = KindReadonlyKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsReadonly
        if k = KindOverrideKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsOverride
        if k = KindInKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsIn
        if k = KindOutKeyword
            return ModifierFlagsOut
        if k = KindDecorator
            return ModifierFlagsDecorator
        ModifierFlagsNone
    }

    ; ── the five predicates checkGrammarModifiers asks (slice 49) ───────────
    ;
    ; ast.IsThisIdentifier and ast.IsThisParameter. The reference asks the second
    ; at the TOP of checkGrammarModifiers, before anything else it might report:
    ; `this` is a parameter in name only and may carry neither a decorator nor a
    ; modifier, so the whole rest of the function would report the wrong thing
    ; about it.
    function is_this_identifier(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if kind_of(n) <> KindIdentifier
            return false
        text_is(n, "this")
    }

    function is_this_parameter(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        if kind_of(n) <> KindParameter
            return false
        is_this_identifier(name_of(n))
    }

    function is_private_identifier(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        kind_of(n) = KindPrivateIdentifier
    }

    ; ast.IsMethodOrAccessor. Three kinds, and NOT the same set as
    ; is_accessor_kind in the checker (which is the reference's ast.IsAccessor,
    ; two kinds) — the two are one apart and are asked in different places, so
    ; they stay separate the way the reference keeps them separate.
    function is_method_or_accessor(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        let k kind_of(n)
        if k = KindMethodDeclaration
            return true
        if k = KindGetAccessor
            return true
        if k = KindSetAccessor
            return true
        false
    }

    ; ast.IsPrivateIdentifierClassElementDeclaration. Read twice by
    ; checkGrammarModifiers — once to reject an accessibility modifier on `#x` and
    ; once to reject `declare` on it — and the name is exact: it is the
    ; DECLARATION whose NAME is a private identifier, not a private identifier
    ; that happens to sit in a class.
    function is_private_identifier_class_element_declaration(n: ref[AstNode]?) returns bool
    {
        if n = null
            return false
        var eligible false
        if kind_of(n) = KindPropertyDeclaration
            set eligible: true
        if is_method_or_accessor(n)
            set eligible: true
        if eligible = false
            return false
        is_private_identifier(name_of(n))
    }
    ; ── the writes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    procedure set_type(n: ref[AstNode], t: ref[AstNode]?)
    {
        choose n.data
            when vd: VariableDeclaration
            {
                var d vd
                set d.type_node: t
                set n.data: NodeData.VariableDeclaration(d)
            }
            when pd: PropertyDeclaration
            {
                var d pd
                set d.type_node: t
                set n.data: NodeData.PropertyDeclaration(d)
            }
            when ps: PropertySignature
            {
                var d ps
                set d.type_node: t
                set n.data: NodeData.PropertySignature(d)
